{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018fa444czt9WdE3PmfCbamA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Experiments}\n2\t\\label{sec:experiments}\n3\t\n4\t\n5\t\\subsection{Statistical Framework}\n6\t\\label{sec:stat_framework}\n7\t\n8\tWe designate a primary family of six hypothesis tests (two judges $\\times$ three style axes) and control the family-wise error rate with Holm-Bonferroni correction at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n9\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_results} reports both raw and adjusted $p$-values for this $2 \\times 3$ grid.\n10\tAnalyses outside this grid are classified as either \\emph{exploratory} (NLI threshold sensitivity sweep, \\S\\ref{sec:nli_threshold}) or \\emph{confirmatory} (consistency filter validation, \\S\\ref{sec:consistency}).\n11\tExploratory analyses are reported with uncorrected $p$-values and interpreted as hypothesis-generating; confirmatory analyses test specific hypotheses about pipeline validity and are evaluated at the nominal $\\alpha = 0.05$ level without additional correction, as they address orthogonal questions about measurement reliability rather than the primary style-bias family.\n12\tBradley-Terry parameters are estimated via spectral initialization followed by minorization-maximization \\citep{hunter2004mm}, using the default regularization of the \\texttt{choix} Python library.\n13\tAll bootstrap confidence intervals use 10{,}000 pair-level resamples with seed 42.\n14\t\n15\t\\begin{table}[t]\n16\t\\centering\n17\t\\small\n18\t\\caption{Main results across three judges, three style axes, and three benchmarks. BT: Bradley-Terry win probability (10{,}000 pair-level bootstrap resamples; 95\\% CIs in Appendix~\\ref{app:bt_ci}). AEval: AlpacaEval; MTB: MT-Bench. Claude Sonnet 4 serves as negative control.}\n19\t\\label{tab:main_results}\n20\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lllccc@{}}\n21\t\\toprule\n22\tBench & Judge & Axis & BT $P$ & Likert $d$ ($p$) & \\\\\n23\t\\midrule\n24\tLLMBar & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .630$^{***}$ & .102 (.332) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n25\t & GPT-4o & Verb.\\ & .760$^{***}$ & $-$.095 (.403) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n26\t & GPT-4o & Reg.\\ & .570$^{\\dagger}$ & $-$.031 (.785) & $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$ \\\\\n27\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n28\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .665$^{***}$ & .011 (.908) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n29\t & Qwen3 & Form.$^b$ & .675$^{***}$ & $-$.025 (.600) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n30\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .773$^{***}$ & $-$.151 (.179) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n31\t & Qwen3 & Reg.\\ & .553 & $-$.073 (.505) & $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$ \\\\\n32\t\\cmidrule{2-6}\n33\t & Claude & Form.\\ & .562 & $-$.021 (.721) & --- \\\\\n34\t & Claude & Verb.\\ & .541 & $-$.039 (.743) & --- \\\\\n35\t\\midrule\n36\tAEval & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .727$^{***}$ & .148 (.170) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n37\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .657$^{***}$ & .081 (.198) & $\\dagger$ \\\\\n38\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .841$^{***}$ & .375 (.0003) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n39\t\\midrule\n40\tMTB & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .692$^{***}$ & .167 (.016) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n41\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .712$^{***}$ & .149 (.007) & $\\ddagger$ \\\\\n42\t\\bottomrule\n43\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $^{***}$Holm-adj.\\ $p < 0.001$;\\; $^{\\dagger}$raw $p < 0.05$, Holm-adj.\\ n.s.}\\\\\n44\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $\\dagger$: Blindspot;\\; $\\ddagger$: both sig.;\\; ---: neither;\\; $\\dagger_{\\text{e}}$: exploratory.}\\\\\n45\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize $^b$ Scaled replication ($n = 355$); other LLMBar: $n = 88$.}\\\\\n46\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize Negative $d$: judge rates style-manipulated variants lower on Likert.}\\\\\n47\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize Empty cells: benchmark--axis combinations not tested; cross-benchmark}\\\\\n48\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\footnotesize validation focuses on strongest-evidence configurations.}\n49\t\\end{tabular}\n50\t\\end{table}\n51\t\n52\t\n53\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n54\t\\centering\n55\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/forest_plot.pdf}\n56\t\\caption{Bradley-Terry win probabilities across two judges and three style axes. Filled diamonds: significant ($p < 0.05$); open diamonds: non-significant. Error bars: 95\\% bootstrap CI. The dashed line at 0.50 indicates no style preference. Verbosity shows the strongest and most consistent bias across both judges.}\n57\t\\label{fig:forest_plot}\n58\t\\end{figure}\n59\t\n60\t\\section{Analysis}\n61\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n62\t\n63\t\n64\t\\subsection{NLI Gate Validation}\n65\t\\label{sec:nli_validation}\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{False negative rate.}\n68\tWe validate all 100 NLI decisions against an LLM annotator (Qwen3-32B) prompted to judge whether semantic content is preserved.\n69\tThe two systems agree on 91 of 100 pairs (Gwet's AC1 $= 0.89$; Cohen's $\\kappa = 0.43$ is depressed by prevalence skew \\citep{feinstein1990kappa}).\n70\tOf the 12 rejected pairs, 8 are judged content-preserving by the LLM annotator, yielding an overall false negative rate of $8/95 = 8.4\\%$.\n71\tThree robustness checks confirm these do not compromise conclusions: win rate stable across NLI thresholds (72.3--73.9\\%, all $p < 0.005$; \\S\\ref{sec:nli_threshold}), no covariate differences between retained and discarded pairs (all $p > 0.12$; \\S\\ref{sec:consistency}), and no directional bias in false negatives (Fisher $p = 0.35$).\n72\tThe NLI gate operates as a precision-oriented conservative filter (precision $= 98.9\\%$); verbosity-rejected pairs have larger edit distances (Cliff's $\\delta = +0.45$, $p < 0.001$), making estimates lower bounds.\n73\tFull-scale GPT-4o validation on all 200 NLI gate decisions yields 92.1\\% pass precision for formality and 82.6\\% for verbosity, with the lower verbosity figure reflecting the permissiveness of direction-aware gating (17.4\\% false positives vs.\\ 7.9\\% for the strict bidirectional formality gate---a trade-off for the higher pass rate that enables adequate sample sizes).\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Threshold sensitivity.}\n76\t\\label{sec:nli_threshold}\n77\tThe formality win rate is stable at 72.3--73.9\\% across NLI thresholds of 0.70, 0.90, and 0.95 (sign test $p < 0.005$ at every threshold).\n78\tNLI scores follow a bimodal distribution (most pairs above 0.95 or below 0.30), so the 0.90 threshold lies on a plateau rather than a cliff edge.\n79\t\n80\t\n81\t\\subsection{Measurement Sensitivity: Likert vs.\\ Pairwise}\n82\t\\label{sec:likert_pairwise}\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Head-to-head comparison with RATE.}\n85\tApplying RATE's Likert ATE methodology \\citep{reber2025rate} to our formality style-transformed pairs yields Cohen's $d = 0.10$, $p = 0.33$ (non-significant, $n = 88$); pairwise comparison on identical data reveals a 63.0\\% formal preference ($p = 0.0004$, Bradley-Terry on all 173 comparisons), confirmed by a consistency-filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$, $n = 46$ consistent pairs).\n86\tThis is the Likert Blindspot: the same data, the same style intervention, two measurement instruments---one detects bias, the other does not.\n87\tA $4\\times$ scaled replication ($n = 355$) distinguishes measurement attenuation from insufficient power: the Likert effect size shrinks to $d = {-}0.025$ (near zero) while BT remains stable at $67.5\\%$ ($p < 0.0001$)---if the gap were merely statistical power, $d$ would remain non-zero at larger $n$.\n88\tAttenuation follows a severity spectrum.\n89\tAt moderate bias (BT 63--67\\%), it is complete: $d$ converges to ${\\approx}0$ even at $n = 355$, rendering the bias invisible to any Likert-based test.\n90\tAt stronger effects (BT ${>}70\\%$), Likert partially detects but severely attenuates: MT-Bench formality yields Likert $d \\approx 0.15$ for BT ${\\sim}70\\%$ effects---${>}60\\%$ signal compression---explaining why three of nine bias-positive cells escape the Blindspot (Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}, $\\ddagger$ rows).\n91\t\n92\tThe two frameworks are complementary: RATE measures aggregate quality effects via Likert ATE; CSD targets per-axis style preference via forced-choice comparison, which eliminates ties and is more sensitive to ordinal preferences.\n93\tThe Blindspot arises because Likert tie rates cluster at 58--73\\% across all six judge--axis configurations---a structural property of integer scales that collapses real preference signal into uninformative ties before any statistical test is applied.\n94\t\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Sensitivity hierarchy across measurement methods.}\n97\tTo confirm that the Likert Blindspot is not an artifact of a particular statistical test, we apply seven measurement methods to the same formality data and rank them by sensitivity (Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity}).\n98\tBoth pairwise methods detect the formality preference; all Likert-based methods fail regardless of whether they assume interval scaling (ATE $t$-test), ordinal scaling (ordinal logistic regression), or nonparametric ranking (Wilcoxon signed-rank).\n99\tThe same attenuation pattern replicates on verbosity (Appendix~\\ref{app:verb_sensitivity}), where all six Likert methods remain non-significant despite a stronger pairwise signal (BT 77.3\\%, $p < 0.001$).\n100\t\n101\t\\begin{table}[t]\n102\t\\centering\n103\t\\small\n104\t\\caption{Sensitivity hierarchy: seven measurement methods applied to the same formality style-transformed data. Only pairwise methods (top two rows) reach significance. All Likert-based methods fail.}\n105\t\\label{tab:sensitivity}\n106\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n107\t\\toprule\n108\tMethod & Statistic & $p$ & Data \\\\\n109\t\\midrule\n110\tBT model & 63.0\\% $[55.7, 70.3]$ & $0.0004$ & All 173 comp. \\\\\n111\tSign test (filtered) & 73.9\\% (34/46) & 0.006 & Consistent \\\\\n112\t\\midrule\n113\tLikert ATE ($t$-test) & $d = 0.102$ & 0.332 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n114\tBinarized Likert & 60.6\\% & 0.296 & Non-tied \\\\\n115\tOrdinal reg.\\ (prop.\\ odds) & $\\beta = {-}0.079$ & 0.769 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n116\tGEE (exchangeable) & $\\beta = {-}0.084$ & 0.587 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n117\tWilcoxon signed-rank & $W = 274$ & 0.905 & NLI$\\geq$0.90 \\\\\n118\t\\bottomrule\n119\t\\end{tabular}\n120\t\\end{table}\n121\t\n122\tFigure~\\ref{fig:power_curve} plots detection probability curves at observed effect magnitudes (not post-hoc power; cf.\\ \\citealt{hoenig2001abuse}): Likert requires ${\\approx}4.5\\times$ more data to detect the same bias.\n123\tAn equally plausible interpretation is that Likert and pairwise modes measure fundamentally different constructs (absolute quality vs.\\ relative preference) rather than reflecting measurement failure; both interpretations imply that Likert-only evaluation misses style-driven preference patterns that matter for deployment.\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Detection vs.\\ amplification.}\n126\t\\label{sec:amplification}\n127\t\\citet{jeong2025comparative} show that pairwise comparison amplifies style biases (the Comparative Trap).\n128\tFour observations bound this risk: (i)~NLI gate ablation shows formality NLI-fail pairs \\emph{reverse} to BT 41.7\\% while NLI-pass pairs show 66.5\\%---the gate filters content noise, not amplifies style signal (Appendix~\\ref{app:nli_ablation}); (ii)~Claude Sonnet 4 shows no bias with the identical pairwise format (BT 56.2\\%/54.1\\%, n.s.); (iii)~the cross-axis gradient (register 55\\% $<$ formality 67\\% $<$ verbosity 77\\%) correlates with manipulation invasiveness rather than uniform amplification; (iv)~debiased prompting reduces Qwen3-32B's BT from 66.5\\% to 60.8\\%, inconsistent with format-fabricated bias.\n129\tCSD cannot fully separate detection from amplification of micro-preferences, but the practical implication is identical.\n130\t\n131\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n132\t\\centering\n133\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/power_curve.pdf}\n134\t\\caption{Detection probability at observed effect magnitudes. 80\\% power crossings: sign test $n \\approx 62$, BT $n \\approx 114$, binarized Likert $n \\approx 462$, Likert ATE $n \\approx 1{,}473$. At typical scales ($n = 50$--$200$, shaded), pairwise methods reach 80\\% power while Likert methods remain far below. At $n = 355$, Likert $d = {-}0.025$ confirms measurement attenuation rather than a mere power gap.}\n135\t\\label{fig:power_curve}\n136\t\\end{figure}\n137\t\n138\t\\subsection{Cross-Judge Replication: Qwen3-32B}\n139\t\\label{sec:cross_judge}\n140\t\n141\tQwen3-32B replicates the Blindspot: BT 66.5\\% ($[58.5\\%, 73.9\\%]$, $p < 0.0001$) while Likert $d = 0.011$ ($p = 0.908$).\n142\tThe higher estimate may partly reflect evaluator-producer bias (Qwen3-32B serves as both rewriter and judge; cross-rewriter validation in Appendix~\\ref{app:rewriter_indep}).\n143\tVerbosity ORs are highly consistent across judges ($11.34$ vs.\\ $13.10$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n144\t\n145\t\\subsection{Cross-Axis Replication: Verbosity}\n146\t\\label{sec:cross_axis}\n147\t\n148\tThe verbosity axis provides the strongest and cleanest demonstration of the Blindspot.\n149\tBoth judges show near-identical verbosity preference: GPT-4o 76.0\\% (BT, bootstrap 95\\% CI: $[66.7\\%, 85.3\\%]$, $p = 0.0001$) and Qwen3-32B 77.3\\% ($[70.3\\%, 84.3\\%]$, $p < 0.0001$), while Likert ATE remains non-significant for both ($d = -0.095$, $p = 0.403$; $d = -0.151$, $p = 0.179$).\n150\tUnlike formality, where the style effect is confounded with originality, verbosity exhibits no observed originality confound for either judge: GEE decomposition confirms massive style effects (Qwen3-32B $\\text{OR} = 13.10$; GPT-4o $\\text{OR} = 11.34$; both $p < .0001$) while the originality component is non-significant in both (Qwen3-32B $\\text{OR} = 0.49$, $p = .096$; GPT-4o $\\text{OR} = 0.45$, $p = .062$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n151\tBoth rewriting directions favor verbose responses, with the concise$\\to$verbose direction (90.7\\%) even exceeding verbose$\\to$concise (80.6\\%), providing convergent directional and GEE evidence that the full 77.3\\% effect is attributable to style rather than originality.\n152\tControlling for length (ratio, difference, log-ratio) leaves the style OR virtually unchanged ($<$2\\%; all $p > 0.27$).\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Register: Weak Evidence with Strong Originality Confound}\n155\t\\label{sec:negative_control}\n156\t\n157\tRegister provides weak but directionally consistent evidence (GPT-4o BT 57.0\\%, $p = 0.04$; Qwen3-32B 55.3\\%, $p = 0.076$; Likert n.s.).\n158\tGEE decomposition reveals the strongest originality confound ($\\text{OR} = 5.72$, $p < 0.0001$) alongside a marginally significant style effect ($\\text{OR} = 1.91$, $p = 0.046$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n159\tThis exploratory finding does not enter the primary Holm-Bonferroni family.\n160\t\n161\t\\subsection{GEE Conditional Decomposition and Confound Structure}\n162\t\\label{sec:direction}\n163\t\n164\tDirection analysis reveals partial confounding between style and original quality across axes; GEE conditional decomposition \\citep{liang1986gee} separates the two effects (full direction-asymmetry analysis in Appendix~\\ref{app:direction}).\n165\tAfter controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant for both judges (GPT-4o: $\\text{OR} = 2.58$, $p = 0.002$; Qwen3-32B: $\\text{OR} = 2.15$, $p < 0.0001$, $n = 355$; Table~\\ref{tab:gee}); the significant originality OR ($2.32$, $p = 0.006$) confirms residual $R \\to Q$ confounding, so this is a partial rather than clean separation.\n166\tA $4\\times$ scaled replication ($n = 355$) confirms the Blindspot: BT stable at $67.5\\%$ (halved CI), Likert still non-significant ($p = 0.60$); the OR decrease ($4.20 \\to 2.15$) reflects small-sample correction.\n167\t\n168\t\\begin{table}[t]\n169\t\\centering\n170\t\\small\n171\t\\caption{GEE conditional decomposition (exchangeable correlation) of style and originality effects. Verbosity shows no observed originality confound; formality and register show comparable style effects with varying originality confounds.}\n172\t\\label{tab:gee}\n173\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llclc@{}}\n174\t\\toprule\n175\t & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Style OR} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Originality OR} \\\\\n176\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n177\tConfig & OR & $p$ & OR & $p$ \\\\\n178\t\\midrule\n179\tQwen3 verb.\\ & 13.10 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.49 & .096 \\\\\n180\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & 11.34 & ${<}.0001$ & 0.45 & .062 \\\\\n181\t\\midrule\n182\tGPT-4o form.\\ & 2.58 & .002 & 2.32 & .006 \\\\\n183\tQwen3 form.\\ & 4.20 & .0002 & 4.70 & .0001 \\\\\n184\tQwen3 form.$^a$ & 2.15 & ${<}.0001$ & 4.99 & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n185\t\\midrule\n186\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & 1.88 & .029 & 2.21 & .006 \\\\\n187\tQwen3 reg.\\ & 1.91 & .046 & 5.72 & ${<}.0001$ \\\\\n188\t\\bottomrule\n189\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\footnotesize $^a$ $n = 355$; other rows: $n = 88$. 95\\% CIs in Appendix.}\n190\t\\end{tabular}\n191\t\\end{table}\n192\t\n193\tThe confound structure reveals two tiers: verbosity shows no observed originality confound (Style OR $= 13.10$ / $11.34$, Originality n.s.); formality and register show comparable style effects (OR $\\approx 1.9$--$2.6$) alongside significant originality confounds.\n194\tCross-benchmark replication confirms formality bias across all six benchmark$\\times$judge configurations (BT 63--73\\%, all $p < 0.001$; AlpacaEval verbosity: BT 84.1\\%, Style OR $= 28.64$); Claude Sonnet 4 shows no style preference (BT 56.2\\%/54.1\\%, n.s.) but extreme originality sensitivity (Orig OR $= 6.66$, $p < 0.0001$).\n195\tThe Blindspot manifests in six of nine bias-positive cells; three exceptions (BT $> 69\\%$, Likert $d \\geq 0.14$) confirm that only extreme effects cross Likert's threshold.\n196\tSame-style double-rewrite controls confirm a rewriting artifact (Appendix~\\ref{app:artifact_control}), validating GEE decomposition: the artifact cancels in GEE's originality term, leaving Style ORs uncontaminated.\n197\tA debiased prompt reduces Qwen3-32B's BT from $66.5\\%$ to $60.8\\%$ ($p = 0.0023$) with negligible GPT-4o effect; prompt-level debiasing is insufficient.\n198\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LygPnDBjmQXz9UNmYayUzR","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"28a60cb0-15db-44bd-a8fd-28b563821379","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:18:29.897Z"}
