{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017V2NPcTXWkcn92LmhU2LAC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\tTwo lines of work motivate our approach: interventional methods for diagnosing judge bias, and measurement theory on the sensitivity of different elicitation formats.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{Interventional Approaches to Judge Bias}\n7\tText-based interventional methods have a growing methodological foundation \\citep{feder2021causalm,wu2021polyjuice,feder2022causal}.\n8\t\\citet{reber2025rate} propose RATE, a framework that rewrites model outputs along style attributes using imperfect counterfactuals and measures the causal effect on reward model scores via Likert-scale Average Treatment Effect (ATE).\n9\tRATE applies a correction term to account for rewriting imperfections, but its reliance on Likert ATE as the primary metric is a vulnerability: our experiments show that Likert scoring exhibits a ceiling effect in which 60\\% of style-transformed pairs receive identical integer scores, yielding non-significant ATE estimates ($p = 0.332$) for the same interventions that pairwise comparison detects at $p = 0.006$.\n10\tIf this Likert Blindspot generalizes beyond our setting, RATE's own style-related findings may underestimate the true magnitude of attribute effects.\n11\tCSD differs from RATE in three respects: it targets individual style axes rather than general attributes, it measures bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison rather than Likert ATE, and it enforces content preservation via NLI entailment gating rather than post-hoc correction for imperfect rewrites.\n12\t\\citet{counterfactual2025rhetorical} also adopt an interventional LLM-based design, rewriting texts to measure rhetorical style effects and aggregating preferences with a Bradley-Terry model.\n13\tTheir framework operates at persona-level granularity (e.g., ``writes like a professor''), whereas CSD intervenes on specific style axes (e.g., formality, conciseness) and uses a sign test with position control rather than parametric aggregation.\n14\t\n15\t\\paragraph{Measurement Sensitivity in NLG Evaluation}\n16\tThe distinction between ordinal and interval measurement dates to \\citet{stevens1946theory}, whose taxonomy of scales remains foundational.\n17\tThe superiority of pairwise comparison over rating scales for eliciting reliable human judgments has been established since \\citet{thurstone1927law}, whose Law of Comparative Judgment formalizes why forced-choice comparisons yield finer discriminability than absolute ratings.\n18\t\\citet{preston2000scale} show that scale granularity affects reliability, while \\citet{krosnick1999survey} documents satisficing behavior that may compound the tie inflation we observe.\n19\t\\citet{decoster2011median} show that artificial categorization of continuous variables discards variance and reduces statistical power, directly relevant to the Likert Blindspot mechanism.\n20\tWithin NLP, \\citet{amidei2019likert} review the use of rating and Likert scales in NLG evaluation, identifying inconsistencies in scale design and reporting that compromise cross-study comparability.\n21\t\\citet{clark2021human} demonstrate systematic flaws in human evaluation; \\citet{celikyilmaz2020evaluation} survey evaluation methods, and \\citet{vanderlee2019best,vanderlee2021human} establish best practices for human NLG evaluation.\n22\t\\citet{kiritchenko2017bws} and \\citet{novikova2018rankme} confirm that forced-choice methods (best-worst scaling, ranking) detect quality differences that rating scales miss; \\citet{louviere2015bws} provide the theoretical foundation.\n23\tOur contribution is the first systematic quantification of this gap for LLM judge style bias, identifying the two-layer mechanism (tie inflation plus residual signal attenuation) that explains it.\n24\t\n25\t\\paragraph{Style Bias Detection and Mitigation}\n26\tThe foundational LLM-as-judge framework was established by \\citet{zheng2023judging}, who introduced MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena and documented position bias in pairwise evaluation.\n27\t\\citet{wang2023faireval} further showed that preference rankings can be manipulated by swapping option order, proposing a win/tie/lose annotation scheme.\n28\t\\citet{li2024calibraeval} address selection bias through calibration of prediction distributions during inference.\n29\tThese works focus on \\emph{position} bias and calibration; \\citet{ye2024justice} systematically quantify 12 bias types using automated counterfactual modifications, and \\citet{koo2024cobbler} benchmark cognitive biases in LLM evaluators, though neither provides per-axis isolation of individual style effects.\n30\tStyle bias as a separate, dominant bias source has received attention only recently \\citep{wu2023style,murugadoss2025sos,li2026preference,soumik2026judging}.\n31\t\\citet{li2026preference} further show that style-mediated \\emph{preference leakage} propagates through the evaluation pipeline, motivating per-axis isolation of style effects.\n32\t\\citet{tan2025judgebench} provide JudgeBench, a benchmark revealing that even strong judges perform near chance on challenging pairs, underscoring the need for controlled experimental designs rather than aggregate accuracy metrics.\n33\tMulti-feature frameworks such as CASSE \\citep{casse2026concise} and FairJudge \\citep{wang2026fairjudge} decompose style into finer dimensions but rely on correlational analyses without interventional controls.\n34\t\\citet{stureborg2024inconsistent} demonstrate inconsistency and bias across evaluation formats, and \\citet{park2024offsetbias} show that verbosity and other surface features systematically inflate judge scores.\n35\tCrucially, \\citet{jeong2025comparative} identify the Comparative Trap: pairwise comparison amplifies style biases relative to pointwise scoring, raising the concern that our pairwise-based detection may partly reflect format-level amplification rather than pure detection.\n36\tWe address this directly in \\S\\ref{sec:amplification}.\n37\t\n38\t\\paragraph{Bradley-Terry Models in Evaluation}\n39\tThe Bradley-Terry model \\citep{bradley1952rank} underpins Chatbot Arena \\citep{zheng2023judging,chiang2024chatbot} and AlpacaFarm \\citep{dubois2023alpacafarm}; \\citet{boubdir2023elo} establish best practices, and \\citet{liusie2024comparative} show zero-shot LLM pairwise comparison matches pointwise evaluation.\n40\tWe adopt BT because it uses all comparison data including inconsistent pairs, yielding proper effect-size CIs; our contribution is to measure the Likert--pairwise sensitivity gap for style bias and show that per-axis GEE decomposition reveals confound structures invisible to aggregate scores.\n41\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LygPnDBjmQXz9UNmYayUzR","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"fb5b716f-d1bc-4e73-bb84-5dc9f7f89644","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:18:36.293Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011rXKc6UidAtRFnXEEqUTSt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1-5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5). Main acceptance ~21%.\n\n## Paper: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n### Abstract\nLLM-as-judge evaluation relies on style-biased judges, but standard Likert evaluation fails to detect this. The paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a black-box framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style manipulation, and position-controlled pairwise comparison. Tested on 5 judges, 3 benchmarks, 3 style axes. Main finding: \"Likert Blindspot\" — pairwise BT detects significant style bias (63-77%) where Likert yields NS results. Placebo controls bound rewriting artifacts to ~5-7pp vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Key claims:\n1. **CSD Method**: Black-box framework for per-axis style bias detection combining NLI-gated content control, style-axis decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n2. **Likert Blindspot**: Measurement attenuation spectrum — Likert fails to detect style biases that pairwise comparison reveals. Detection only when BT >~80%.\n3. **Axis-stratified bias**: Verbosity = pure confound-free effect (3/3 judges), formality = conditional effect (2/3), register = originality-dominated.\n4. **Bounded amplification**: Placebo controls show ~5-7pp rewriting artifact vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Related Work (Section 2, brief)\nPaper positions against:\n- **SOS-Bench** (Sun et al., 2024): R=0.999 style-preference correlation, but correlational only\n- **FairJudge** (Chen et al., 2024): fairness-aware judging but no per-axis isolation\n- **RATE** (Eisenstein et al., 2024): Rubin potential outcomes + Likert ATE on semi-synthetic — paper claims to show Likert measurement instrument itself is the problem\n- **Counterfactual Rhetorical** (Salinas et al., 2024): perturb rhetorical features, measure judgment shifts\n- **Comparative Trap** (Jeong et al., 2025): concern that pairwise amplifies biases — paper addresses with placebo\n- **LC-AlpacaEval** (Dubois et al., 2024): length deconfounding via regression, single axis\n- **CASSE** (Zhou et al., 2025): causal assessment of style and substance, but correlational/no per-axis causal\n- **Wu and Aji (2024)**: style influence across multiple dimensions\n- **Thurstone (1927)**: law of comparative judgment (pairwise > absolute for small differences)\n\n### Research landscape context (from domain knowledge):\nKey competitors and their claims:\n- **Preference Leakage (ICLR'26)**: Model family relationships leak preferences through style; contextual calibration reduces 59% Error Bias\n- **Soumik (2026)**: CoT is only universally safe strategy; debiasing is model-dependent\n- **FairJudge (2026)**: Training-side 3-stage approach (SFT→DPO→GRPO), 8B beats 72B/671B\n- **LC-AlpacaEval (ICLR'25)**: Length as causal mediator, regression debiasing\n- **SOS-Bench (ICLR'25)**: Style-overall R=0.999\n- **RATE (ICML'25)**: Double-rewrite eliminates counterfactual systematic error\n- **CASSE (2026)**: Style feature causal entanglement, mitigation faces alignment tax\n\nResearch gaps this paper targets:\n1. Multi-dimensional style causal decomposition (no prior work does joint per-axis causal decomposition)\n2. Measurement instrument comparison (Likert vs pairwise) for style bias detection\n\n### Method details\n- 5 components: counterfactual generation, NLI-gated content control (τ=0.90), position-controlled pairwise comparison, Bradley-Terry modeling, GEE confound decomposition\n- 5 judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Gemini-2.0-Flash, Llama-3.3-70B, Claude Sonnet 4\n- 3 benchmarks: LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench\n- 3 axes: verbosity, formality, register\n\n### Results highlights\n- Table 1: Verbosity 3/3 judges biased (BT 66-77%), Formality 3/4 biased (Llama NS). All Likert NS.\n- Table 3 GEE: Verbosity = pure style effect; Formality = conditional (style+originality both sig); Register = originality-dominated\n- Table 5: Placebo BT 54.8-57.1%, amplification fraction 24-42%\n- Table 6: 3 rewriters produce consistent BT (63.8-66.5%), NLI ablation shows finding robust to gating\n\n### Your task\nWrite a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is CSD relative to RATE, CASSE, LC-AlpacaEval, Counterfactual Rhetorical? Is the \"Likert Blindspot\" a genuinely new finding or an expected consequence of Thurstone's law?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important competitors missing? Is positioning fair to prior work?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the 4 claimed contributions individually and collectively sufficient for EMNLP Main? For Findings?\n4. **Narrative quality**: Is the paper well-written? Are claims appropriately hedged?\n5. **Missing comparisons**: Should the paper compare more directly against RATE's methodology? Against CASSE? Against Preference Leakage?\n\nOutput format — produce a structured review:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, specific)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, half-step)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards (~21% Main acceptance). The Likert Blindspot is a catchy name — evaluate whether the substance matches the framing."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33826,"cache_read_input_tokens":23947,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":33826},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"2a1cea41-b9e6-4794-8f51-c2a1669b1bee"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a12130b52692934ee","tool_use_id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1-5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5). Main acceptance ~21%.\n\n## Paper: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n### Abstract\nLLM-as-judge evaluation relies on style-biased judges, but standard Likert evaluation fails to detect this. The paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a black-box framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style manipulation, and position-controlled pairwise comparison. Tested on 5 judges, 3 benchmarks, 3 style axes. Main finding: \"Likert Blindspot\" — pairwise BT detects significant style bias (63-77%) where Likert yields NS results. Placebo controls bound rewriting artifacts to ~5-7pp vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Key claims:\n1. **CSD Method**: Black-box framework for per-axis style bias detection combining NLI-gated content control, style-axis decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n2. **Likert Blindspot**: Measurement attenuation spectrum — Likert fails to detect style biases that pairwise comparison reveals. Detection only when BT >~80%.\n3. **Axis-stratified bias**: Verbosity = pure confound-free effect (3/3 judges), formality = conditional effect (2/3), register = originality-dominated.\n4. **Bounded amplification**: Placebo controls show ~5-7pp rewriting artifact vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Related Work (Section 2, brief)\nPaper positions against:\n- **SOS-Bench** (Sun et al., 2024):