 Bradley-Terry modeling, GEE confound decomposition\n- 5 judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Gemini-2.0-Flash, Llama-3.3-70B, Claude Sonnet 4\n- 3 benchmarks: LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench\n- 3 axes: verbosity, formality, register\n\n### Results highlights\n- Table 1: Verbosity 3/3 judges biased (BT 66-77%), Formality 3/4 biased (Llama NS). All Likert NS.\n- Table 3 GEE: Verbosity = pure style effect; Formality = conditional (style+originality both sig); Register = originality-dominated\n- Table 5: Placebo BT 54.8-57.1%, amplification fraction 24-42%\n- Table 6: 3 rewriters produce consistent BT (63.8-66.5%), NLI ablation shows finding robust to gating\n\n### Your task\nWrite a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is CSD relative to RATE, CASSE, LC-AlpacaEval, Counterfactual Rhetorical? Is the \"Likert Blindspot\" a genuinely new finding or an expected consequence of Thurstone's law?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important competitors missing? Is positioning fair to prior work?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the 4 claimed contributions individually and collectively sufficient for EMNLP Main? For Findings?\n4. **Narrative quality**: Is the paper well-written? Are claims appropriately hedged?\n5. **Missing comparisons**: Should the paper compare more directly against RATE's methodology? Against CASSE? Against Preference Leakage?\n\nOutput format — produce a structured review:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, specific)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, half-step)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards (~21% Main acceptance). The Likert Blindspot is a catchy name — evaluate whether the substance matches the framing.","uuid":"69b741c1-8732-4aef-9710-e859ae15317a","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1-5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5). Main acceptance ~21%.\n\n## Paper: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n### Abstract\nLLM-as-judge evaluation relies on style-biased judges, but standard Likert evaluation fails to detect this. The paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a black-box framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style manipulation, and position-controlled pairwise comparison. Tested on 5 judges, 3 benchmarks, 3 style axes. Main finding: \"Likert Blindspot\" — pairwise BT detects significant style bias (63-77%) where Likert yields NS results. Placebo controls bound rewriting artifacts to ~5-7pp vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Key claims:\n1. **CSD Method**: Black-box framework for per-axis style bias detection combining NLI-gated content control, style-axis decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n2. **Likert Blindspot**: Measurement attenuation spectrum — Likert fails to detect style biases that pairwise comparison reveals. Detection only when BT >~80%.\n3. **Axis-stratified bias**: Verbosity = pure confound-free effect (3/3 judges), formality = conditional effect (2/3), register = originality-dominated.\n4. **Bounded amplification**: Placebo controls show ~5-7pp rewriting artifact vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Related Work (Section 2, brief)\nPaper positions against:\n- **SOS-Bench** (Sun et al., 2024): R=0.999 style-preference correlation, but correlational only\n- **FairJudge** (Chen et al., 2024): fairness-aware judging but no per-axis isolation\n- **RATE** (Eisenstein et al., 2024): Rubin potential outcomes + Likert ATE on semi-synthetic — paper claims to show Likert measurement instrument itself is the problem\n- **Counterfactual Rhetorical** (Salinas et al., 2024): perturb rhetorical features, measure judgment shifts\n- **Comparative Trap** (Jeong et al., 2025): concern that pairwise amplifies biases — paper addresses with placebo\n- **LC-AlpacaEval** (Dubois et al., 2024): length deconfounding via regression, single axis\n- **CASSE** (Zhou et al., 2025): causal assessment of style and substance, but correlational/no per-axis causal\n- **Wu and Aji (2024)**: style influence across multiple dimensions\n- **Thurstone (1927)**: law of comparative judgment (pairwise > absolute for small differences)\n\n### Research landscape context (from domain knowledge):\nKey competitors and their claims:\n- **Preference Leakage (ICLR'26)**: Model family relationships leak preferences through style; contextual calibration reduces 59% Error Bias\n- **Soumik (2026)**: CoT is only universally safe strategy; debiasing is model-dependent\n- **FairJudge (2026)**: Training-side 3-stage approach (SFT→DPO→GRPO), 8B beats 72B/671B\n- **LC-AlpacaEval (ICLR'25)**: Length as causal mediator, regression debiasing\n- **SOS-Bench (ICLR'25)**: Style-overall R=0.999\n- **RATE (ICML'25)**: Double-rewrite eliminates counterfactual systematic error\n- **CASSE (2026)**: Style feature causal entanglement, mitigation faces alignment tax\n\nResearch gaps this paper targets:\n1. Multi-dimensional style causal decomposition (no prior work does joint per-axis causal decomposition)\n2. Measurement instrument comparison (Likert vs pairwise) for style bias detection\n\n### Method details\n- 5 components: counterfactual generation, NLI-gated content control (τ=0.90), position-controlled pairwise comparison, Bradley-Terry modeling, GEE confound decomposition\n- 5 judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Gemini-2.0-Flash, Llama-3.3-70B, Claude Sonnet 4\n- 3 benchmarks: LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench\n- 3 axes: verbosity, formality, register\n\n### Results highlights\n- Table 1: Verbosity 3/3 judges biased (BT 66-77%), Formality 3/4 biased (Llama NS). All Likert NS.\n- Table 3 GEE: Verbosity = pure style effect; Formality = conditional (style+originality both sig); Register = originality-dominated\n- Table 5: Placebo BT 54.8-57.1%, amplification fraction 24-42%\n- Table 6: 3 rewriters produce consistent BT (63.8-66.5%), NLI ablation shows finding robust to gating\n\n### Your task\nWrite a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is CSD relative to RATE, CASSE, LC-AlpacaEval, Counterfactual Rhetorical? Is the \"Likert Blindspot\" a genuinely new finding or an expected consequence of Thurstone's law?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important competitors missing? Is positioning fair to prior work?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the 4 claimed contributions individually and collectively sufficient for EMNLP Main? For Findings?\n4. **Narrative quality**: Is the paper well-written? Are claims appropriately hedged?\n5. **Missing comparisons**: Should the paper compare more directly against RATE's methodology? Against CASSE? Against Preference Leakage?\n\nOutput format — produce a structured review:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, specific)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, half-step)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards (~21% Main acceptance). The Likert Blindspot is a catchy name — evaluate whether the substance matches the framing."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"37807b9e-44f1-4a04-b878-fc898dd65f2c","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:18:36.803Z"}
