{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0182BdAkNdwexrWUo8vNDKgf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013MZ2v4EsbJbH8AjU9W7YQc","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"causal_style_decomp_judge","file":"paper_framing.md","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1290,"cache_read_input_tokens":16539,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1290,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"586978c7-3128-4419-8688-b86189f572c2"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013MZ2v4EsbJbH8AjU9W7YQc","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — causal_style_decomp_judge\\\\n\\\\n> Round 5: **3.5 Borderline Conference** (up from 3.0). 冲刺 4.0 中。\\\\n> 补充实验进度：B' placebo ✅ + NLI ablation ✅ + cross-axis gradient ✅ + Llama-70B 🔄 downloading。\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\\\\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\\\\n- Deadline: **May 25, 2026 AoE**\\\\n- Format: Long paper, 8-page main text + unlimited references\\\\n\\\\n## Title\\\\n**The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition**\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims (Round 5 stratified)\\\\n\\\\n### C1 (Method — application contribution): CSD pipeline detects per-axis style bias in black-box LLM judges\\\\n- NLI-gated content control + per-axis style decomposition + position-controlled pairwise comparison\\\\n- **NOT** \\\\\\\"approximates the do-operator\\\\\\\" — reframed as \\\\\\\"filtering-based confounder control\\\\\\\" (Round 5 MF2)\\\\n- 3 target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B [pending]) + 1 negative control (Claude Sonnet 4)\\\\n- 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) × 3 axes (formality, verbosity, register)\\\\n\\\\n### C2 (Measurement Insight — 主贡献): Likert Blindspot — measurement attenuation spectrum\\\\n- **Per-axis claim stratification** (Round 5):\\\\n  - **Verbosity** = \\\\\\\"clean style bias\\\\\\\" — BT 77%, GEE Orig OR NS, confound-free. 7-method sensitivity hierarchy confirms 6/6 Likert NS.\\\\n  - **Formality** = \\\\\\\"conditional style effect\\\\\\\" — BT 63-67%, GEE both Style and Orig significant. Moderated by content quality changes.\\\\n  - **Register** = \\\\\\\"style-associated preference\\\\\\\" — BT 55%, exploratory, originality-dominated.\\\\n- Attenuation spectrum: BT 63-67% → complete attenuation (d→0); BT 77% → still complete; BT >80% → Likert starts detecting\\\\n- **Detection vs. Amplification** (Round 5 MF1 — Comparative Trap defense):\\\\n  - Layer 1: **NLI gate ablation** — formality NLI-fail BT reverses to 41.7% (prefer casual), proving gate cleans content noise not amplifies style. Verbosity NLI-fail BT stable 75% (style too strong to be affected).\\\\n  - Layer 2: Claude negative control (same format, no bias), debiased prompt partial reduction, cross-axis gradient (Register 6%/55.3% → Formality 12%/66.5% → Verbosity 14%/77.3%) inconsistent with uniform format amplification\\\\n  - Layer 3: **Rewriting artifact acknowledged** — placebo (orig vs DR) shows judges prefer polished text (formality DR win 68-73%, verbosity 94-100%). GEE decomposition controls for this: Verbosity Orig OR NS → style effect survives artifact control.\\\\n  - Layer 4: Honest framing — \\\\\\\"controlled detection with rewriting artifact quantified and controlled via GEE\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n### C3 (Empirical): Style bias detectability varies by axis and judge\\\\n- GEE confound gradient: Verbosity (pure) > Formality (mixed) > Register (originality-dominated)\\\\n- Judge-dependent: GPT-4o + Qwen3-32B show bias; Claude does not\\\\n- **Pending**: Llama-3.3-70B as third target judge\\\\n\\\\n### C4 (Practical): Practitioner guidance\\\\n- Use content-controlled pairwise comparison (with NLI gating) for style bias audits\\\\n- Per-axis decomposition reveals axis-specific confound structure\\\\n- Prompt-level debiasing insufficient (Qwen3 -5.7pp still sig, GPT-4o -0.4pp)\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence Matrix (Current)\\\\n\\\\n### Primary (LLMBar)\\\\n| | GPT-4o BT / Likert | Qwen3-32B BT / Likert | Claude BT / Likert | Llama-70B (pending) |\\\\n|---|-----|------|------|------|\\\\n| **Formality** | 63.0% p<.001 / p=.332 | 66.5% p<.0001 / p=.908 | 56.2% p=.091 / p=.721 | TBD |\\\\n| **Verbosity** | 76.0% p=.0001 / p=.403 | 77.3% p<.0001 / p=.179 | 54.1% p=.284 / p=.743 | TBD |\\\\n| **Register** | 57.0% p=.04 / p=.785 | 55.3% p=.076 / p=.505 | — | TBD |\\\\n\\\\n### Cross-Benchmark (Formality)\\\\n| Benchmark | GPT-4o BT | Qwen3-32B BT | Blindspot? |\\\\n|-----------|-----------|-------------|------------|\\\\n| LLMBar | 63.0% | 66.5% | Yes |\\\\n| AlpacaEval | 72.7% | 65.7% | Yes |\\\\n| MT-Bench | 69.2% | 71.2% | No (d≈0.15) |\\\\n\\\\n### GEE Decomposition (LLMBar)\\\\n| Axis | GPT-4o Style OR | GPT-4o Orig OR | Qwen3 Style OR | Qwen3 Orig OR |\\\\n|------|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\\\\n| Verbosity | **11.34** p<.0001 | 0.45 NS | **13.10** p<.0001 | 0.49 NS |\\\\n| Formality | 2.58 p=.002 | 2.32 p=.006 | 4.20 p=.0002 | 4.70 p<.0001 |\\\\n| Register | 1.88 p=.029 | 2.21 p=.006 | 1.91 p=.046 | 5.72 p<.0001 |\\\\n\\\\n## Key Robustness Results\\\\n- **BT GoF**: formality χ² p=0.198, verbosity p=0.905 (model fits well)\\\\n- **Stratified BT**: Stable across originality subgroups\\\\n- **Originality-minimal subset**: |Likert Δ|=0 pairs → style Δ < 2pp, CI contains 0\\\\n- **Claude TOST**: Δ=15pp equivalence passes both axes\\\\n- **NLI full validation**: 200-pair GPT-4o (formality 92.1%, verbosity 82.6%)\\\\n- **Rewriter independence**: GPT-4o formality BT 64.4% vs Qwen3 66.5% (Δ=−2.1pp)\\\\n- **Sensitivity hierarchy**: 7 Likert methods × 2 axes (formality + verbosity) all NS, only BT detects\\\\n- **Length robustness**: Verbosity Style OR survives all length controls (<2% change)\\\\n- **NLI ablation** (NEW): Formality NLI-fail BT 41.7% (reversal → prefer casual); Verbosity NLI-fail 75% (stable)\\\\n- **Cross-axis gradient** (NEW): NLI rejection rate vs BT positive correlation across 3 axes\\\\n- **Rewriting artifact control** (NEW): Placebo orig-vs-DR quantifies artifact; GEE Orig OR captures it. Verbosity Orig OR NS = style effect uncontaminated.\\\\n\\\\n## Completed Supplementary Experiments\\\\n\\\\n### B': Double-Rewrite Placebo BT ✅ (hypothesis falsified — artifact exists)\\\\n- Result (BT re-analysis, choix.ilsr_pairwise + ALL pairs + bootstrap 10000):\\\\n  - GPT-4o: Formality P(orig)=0.418 p=.037, Verbosity P(orig)=0.167 p<.0001\\\\n  - Qwen3-32B: Formality P(orig)=0.377 p=.0006, Verbosity P(orig)=0.082 p<.0001\\\\n  - → Judges prefer DR (polished text) over original in all conditions\\\\n- Interpretation: Rewriting artifact is real (judges prefer LLM-polished text). This does NOT invalidate style bias conclusions.\\\\n- Paper role: Validates GEE decomposition as necessary for correct inference. Honest acknowledgment of artifact.\\\\n- GEE controls for it: Verbosity Orig OR=0.49 NS → style effect uncontaminated by artifact.\\\\n\\\\n### B: NLI Gate Ablation ✅ (strong new evidence)\\\\n- Formality: NLI-fail BT reverses to 41.7% (prefer casual) → NLI gate cleans content noise, not amplifies style\\\\n- Verbosity: NLI-fail BT stable at 75% → style effect too strong to be affected by content noise\\\\n- Paper role: Strongest anti-amplification evidence. If pairwise amplified bias, removing the gate should amplify further — instead it reverses.\\\\n\\\\n### C: Cross-Axis Gradient ✅ (qualitative support)\\\\n- NLI rejection rate: Register 6% → Formality 12% → Verbosity 14%\\\\n- BT: Register 55.3% → Formality 66.5% → Verbosity 77.3%\\\\n- Positive correlation: higher manipulation invasiveness → higher BT → inconsistent with uniform amplification\\\\n- Paper role: Qualitative argument in §5.2, 3 points only.\\\\n\\\\n## Pending Experiments\\\\n\\\\n### A: Llama-3.3-70B Third Target Judge 🔄 (downloading, ~4h remaining)\\\\n- Purpose: 2→3 target judges = generalizability quality jump\\\\n- Coverage: verbosity + formality on LLMBar\\\\n- Impact: Directly addresses SF3 + strengthens MF1 defense\\\\n- Three independent architectures: GPT-4o (proprietary US) + Qwen3-32B (open CN) + Llama (open US)\\\\n\\\\n## Novelty Positioning (honest)\\\\n\\\\\\\"The first framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to systematically quantify style bias in LLM judges.\\\\\\\"\\\\n- Domain transfer of Thurstone 1927 / Stevens 1946 — acknowledged\\\\n- Incremental contribution from method execution quality (per-axis + NLI + 7-method hierarchy)\\\\n- Jeong et al. 2025 (Comparative Trap) discusses amplification; we discuss controlled detection\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine\\\\n\\\\n### Problem\\\\nLLM-as-judge is core infrastructure for NLP evaluation and alignment. Style bias is the dominant bias (SOS-Bench R=0.999), but standard Likert scoring systematically misses it.\\\\n\\\\n### Gap (integrative, not cherry-picked)\\\\nPrior work addresses parts of the problem: RATE (Likert-based ATE), CASSE (multi-dimensional but correlational), LC-AlpacaEval (single dimension). CSD integrates NLI-gated content control + per-axis decomposition + pairwise comparison — no prior work combines all three.\\\\n\\\\n### Our Contribution\\\\nControlled Style Decomposition (CSD): a black-box framework for per-axis style bias detection. Core finding: Likert Blindspot — measurement attenuation across a severity spectrum.\\\\n\\\\n### Detection vs. Amplification (Round 5 addition)\\\\nJeong et al. (2025) show pairwise amplifies bias. Our evidence chain:\\\\n1. **NLI ablation**: Removing gate reverses formality BT (41.7%) → gate cleans content, not amplifies style\\\\n2. **Claude negative control**: Same pairwise format, no bias detected → format alone doesn't create bias\\\\n3. **Cross-axis gradient**: Manipulation invasiveness correlates with BT → inconsistent with uniform amplification\\\\n4. **Rewriting artifact acknowledged**: Placebo quantifies artifact, GEE decomposes and controls for it\\\\n→ \\\\\\\"controlled detection with rewriting artifact quantified and controlled\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Bibliography\\\\n50 verified references including Jeong 2025, Stureborg 2024, Park 2024.\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit\\\\nRound 5: 3.5 Borderline Conference. Path to 4.0: MF1 (Comparative Trap — now with NLI ablation + placebo + gradient evidence chain) + 3rd judge (Llama pending).\\\\nNovelty ceiling (domain transfer) is structural. Statistical methodology depth + honest artifact handling are primary strengths.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 9251, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"c878edd7-5647-4113-bc20-4762a049ecc2","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:18:45.208Z"}
