{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FhAMUoHb1n3n3eR7VcSzza","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[review]{acl}\n4\t\n5\t\\usepackage{times}\n6\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n7\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n8\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n9\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n10\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n11\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n12\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n13\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n14\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n15\t\\usepackage{array}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Detecting Architecture-Dependent Style Bias in LLM Judges\\\\via Controlled Style Decomposition}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{Anonymous}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tLLM-as-judge evaluation is central to NLP benchmarking and model alignment, yet style biases---systematic preferences for surface-level textual properties such as verbosity or formality---can silently corrupt evaluation scores. Standard Likert-based auditing fails to detect these biases due to measurement attenuation. We introduce \\textbf{Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a black-box framework that combines NLI-gated content control, per-axis style manipulation, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to produce per-judge, per-axis bias profiles. Applying CSD to four LLM judges across three benchmarks and three style axes, we find that style bias is \\emph{architecture-dependent}: GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B exhibit significant verbosity and formality biases (corrected Bradley--Terry win rates of 65--68\\% for verbosity), while Claude~Sonnet~4 and Llama-3.3-70B show no detectable bias, confirmed by TOST equivalence tests. Crucially, Likert scoring detects none of these biases---a phenomenon we term the \\textbf{Likert Blindspot}. Double-rewrite placebo controls reveal that pairwise amplification is itself architecture-dependent (8--11pp for biased judges, 2--4pp for unbiased ones), and we report conservative corrected estimates throughout. CSD is the first framework to diagnose which judges are susceptible to which style dimensions, providing actionable per-judge auditing for practitioners deploying LLM evaluators.\n26\t\\end{abstract}\n27\t\n28\t\\section{Introduction}\n29\t\n30\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become a cornerstone of NLP evaluation pipelines \\cite{zheng2024judging,dubois2024alpacafarm}. Judges like GPT-4o power leaderboards (Chatbot Arena, AlpacaEval), reward modeling, and alignment feedback loops. When judges harbor systematic style biases---preferring verbose, formal, or polished responses regardless of content quality---downstream evaluations and training signals are corrupted.\n31\t\n32\tPrior work has established that style biases exist in LLM judges \\cite{wu2024style,park2024offsetbias,stureborg2024large}. However, two critical gaps remain. First, standard Likert-based auditing---the default approach for measuring judge quality---systematically fails to detect style biases of moderate magnitude, a phenomenon we formalize as the \\textbf{Likert Blindspot}. Second, existing studies treat style bias as a monolithic property of the pairwise evaluation format, rather than diagnosing \\emph{which judges} are susceptible to \\emph{which style dimensions}.\n33\t\n34\tWe introduce \\textbf{Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a black-box framework for per-axis style bias detection in LLM judges. CSD operates in three stages: (1)~generating style-manipulated counterfactual pairs along isolated axes (formality, verbosity, register) using LLM rewriting, (2)~filtering pairs through NLI-based content equivalence gating to remove content-contaminated manipulations, and (3)~evaluating filtered pairs via position-controlled pairwise comparison and Bradley--Terry modeling. The result is a per-judge, per-axis bias profile that reveals architecture-dependent susceptibility patterns invisible to Likert auditing.\n35\t\n36\tOur key findings are:\n37\t\n38\t\\begin{itemize}\n39\t\\item \\textbf{Architecture-dependent bias.} Of four judges tested, only GPT-4o and Qwen3-32B exhibit significant style bias; Claude~Sonnet~4 and Llama-3.3-70B do not. This 2/4 split is consistent across three benchmarks and confirmed by TOST equivalence testing for the unbiased judges.\n40\t\n41\t\\item \\textbf{The Likert Blindspot.} Across the complete judge$\\times$axis matrix, 83\\% of biased cells are detected only by pairwise comparison, never by Likert scoring---regardless of the Likert variant used (7 methods tested).\n42\t\n43\t\\item \\textbf{Honest amplification accounting.} Double-rewrite placebo controls show 8--11pp pairwise amplification for biased judges but only 2--4pp for unbiased ones. We report corrected Bradley--Terry estimates throughout: verbosity bias remains strong (65--68\\%), formality bias is moderate to marginal (53--58\\%).\n44\t\n45\t\\item \\textbf{Per-judge auditing.} CSD is the first framework to produce per-judge, per-axis bias profiles, enabling practitioners to diagnose whether their specific judge is susceptible before deployment.\n46\t\\end{itemize}\n47\t\n48\t\\section{Related Work}\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{Style bias in LLM evaluation.} Multiple studies document that LLM judges prefer verbose \\cite{saito2023verbosity,wu2024style}, formal, or well-formatted responses \\cite{park2024offsetbias,stureborg2024large}. SOS-Bench \\cite{ye2024sosbench} demonstrates that superficial style features explain $R=0.999$ of variance across evaluation benchmarks. However, these studies primarily diagnose bias at the benchmark level rather than the individual judge level, and none provide per-axis decomposition with content control.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Pairwise vs.\\ Likert evaluation.} The LLM evaluation literature has debated the relative merits of Likert scoring and pairwise comparison \\cite{zheng2024judging}. Jeong et al.\\ \\shortcite{jeong2025comparative} formalize the ``Comparative Trap,'' showing that pairwise formats amplify pre-existing biases. Our work complements this finding: we show that amplification is architecture-dependent and provide placebo-controlled estimates of the amplification floor.\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{Content-controlled evaluation.} RATE \\cite{ye2024rate} introduces average treatment effect estimation for style bias but relies on Likert scoring, which we show systematically attenuates moderate effects. CASSE \\cite{wu2024casse} provides multi-dimensional style analysis but uses correlational rather than interventional designs. LC-AlpacaEval \\cite{dubois2024length} controls for length in a single dimension. CSD integrates NLI-gated content control with per-axis decomposition and pairwise comparison---no prior work combines all three.\n55\t\n56\t\\section{Method: Controlled Style Decomposition}\n57\t\n58\tCSD is a three-stage pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in black-box LLM judges.\n59\t\n60\t\\subsection{Stage 1: Counterfactual Generation}\n61\t\n62\tGiven a source response $r$ and a target style axis $a \\in \\{\\text{formality}, \\text{verbosity}, \\text{register}\\}$, we generate counterfactual pairs $(r, r'_a)$ where $r'_a$ is a style-manipulated version of $r$ along axis $a$. The rewriter LLM receives a structured prompt specifying: (i)~the target style direction (e.g., formal$\\to$casual), (ii)~explicit instructions to preserve semantic content, factual claims, and argument structure, and (iii)~constraints on manipulation scope. Each pair is generated in both directions (e.g., formal$\\to$casual and casual$\\to$formal) to enable direction-balanced analysis.\n63\t\n64\tTo ensure results are not artifacts of a specific rewriter, we validate with three independent rewriter families (Qwen3-32B, GPT-4o, Llama-3.1-70B), confirming that bias estimates are stable across rewriters ($\\Delta < 3.8$pp, all CIs overlapping; Table~\\ref{tab:rewriter}).\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Stage 2: NLI-Based Content Gating}\n67\t\n68\tStyle manipulation inevitably risks altering content. We gate each pair through an NLI model: given $(r, r'_a)$, we verify bidirectional entailment ($r \\models r'_a$ and $r'_a \\models r$). Pairs failing this gate are excluded. Pass rates range from 82--92\\% depending on axis and rewriter, with verbosity having the highest rejection rate (14\\%) due to the inherent difficulty of changing length without altering information density.\n69\t\n70\t\\subsection{Stage 3: Position-Controlled Pairwise Evaluation}\n71\t\n72\tEach NLI-passed pair is presented to the target judge in both orderings (A/B and B/A), yielding position-controlled preference judgments. We fit a Bradley--Terry (BT) model to estimate the win probability $P(\\text{styled} \\succ \\text{original})$, with $P = 0.50$ indicating no bias. For Likert comparison, each response is independently scored on a 5-point scale and we compute the average treatment effect (ATE) as the mean Likert difference.\n73\t\n74\t\\subsection{Amplification Correction}\n75\t\n76\tPairwise comparison may amplify preferences through the rewriting process itself. To quantify this, we construct \\textbf{placebo pairs}: original responses paired with double-rewrites that traverse the style axis and return (e.g., formal$\\to$casual$\\to$formal), introducing rewriting artifacts without net style change. The placebo BT estimates the amplification floor. We define:\n77\t\\begin{equation}\n78\t\\text{BT}_{\\text{corrected}} = \\text{BT}_{\\text{observed}} - (\\text{BT}_{\\text{placebo}} - 0.50)\n79\t\\end{equation}\n80\tThis is a conservative lower bound: it attributes \\emph{all} placebo preference to artifact rather than genuine quality improvement from rewriting polish.\n81\t\n82\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Judges.} Four target judges spanning different architectures and training paradigms: \\textbf{GPT-4o} (OpenAI, RLHF with synthetic preference data), \\textbf{Qwen3-32B} (Alibaba, large-scale synthetic preference training), \\textbf{Claude Sonnet~4} (Anthropic, constitutional AI + RLHF), and \\textbf{Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct} (Meta, RLHF with human annotator preferences). We additionally evaluate Gemini-2.0-Flash as a fifth judge in Appendix~\\ref{app:gemini}.\n85\t\n86\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} Three evaluation benchmarks: \\textbf{LLMBar} \\cite{zeng2024llmbar} (discriminative evaluation, $n=100$ base pairs), \\textbf{AlpacaEval} \\cite{dubois2024alpacafarm} (open-ended instruction following), and \\textbf{MT-Bench} \\cite{zheng2024judging} (multi-turn dialogue).\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Style axes.} Three axes of increasing manipulation invasiveness: \\textbf{verbosity} (length and detail level), \\textbf{formality} (academic vs.\\ conversational register), and \\textbf{register} (technical jargon vs.\\ plain language). NLI rejection rates reflect this ordering: verbosity 14\\%, formality 11.2\\%, register 6\\%.\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Scale.} Primary experiments use $n=100$ pairs per cell (88--90 after NLI gating). We validate precision with a scaled replication at $n=400$ for one key cell (Qwen3-32B formality on LLMBar).\n91\t\n92\t\\section{Results}\n93\t\n94\t\\subsection{Architecture-Dependent Style Bias}\n95\t\\label{sec:main_results}\n96\t\n97\tTable~\\ref{tab:main} presents the complete four-judge evidence matrix for the two primary style axes on LLMBar. The results reveal a clear architecture-dependent split.\n98\t\n99\t\\begin{table}[t]\n100\t\\centering"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LygPnDBjmQXz9UNmYayUzR","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"9012d178-38fe-4902-ac70-99261d6ae82a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:18:50.923Z"}
