{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ptn4UzkHhpsMB1AJ9UCc5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\appendix\n2\t\n3\t\\section{Direction Asymmetry and Quality Confounding}\n4\t\\label{app:direction}\n5\t\n6\tThe formality axis exhibits a pronounced direction asymmetry: the judge selects the formal variant in 78.8\\% of formal-is-rewrite pairs ($p = 0.001$) but only 54.5\\% of casual-is-rewrite pairs ($p = 0.83$).\n7\tThis asymmetry suggests that original quality $Q$ and formality $S$ are partially confounded---formally written texts may be inherently perceived as higher quality.\n8\tTo quantify this, we fit a GEE logistic model (exchangeable correlation) with both formality and original-preference as predictors.\n9\tAfter controlling for original preference, the formality effect remains significant ($\\text{OR} = 2.58$, $p = 0.002$), and the original-preference effect is also significant ($\\text{OR} = 2.32$, $p = 0.006$).\n10\tThe formality preference thus survives adjustment for original-preference confounding, though the significant original-preference effect indicates that quality--style confounding is present and non-negligible.\n11\t\n12\tAt $n = 88$, the Qwen3-32B formality Style OR ($4.20$) exceeded the GPT-4o estimate ($2.58$), but a scaled replication ($n = 355$) corrects this to $2.15$ ($p < 0.0001$), comparable to GPT-4o and consistent with small-sample upward bias rather than evaluator-producer inflation of the style effect.\n13\tThe originality confound remains stronger ($4.99$ vs.\\ $2.32$), consistent with evaluator-producer bias inflating the originality component (Limitations).\n14\tThe directional agreement between judges---both favor formal text after controlling for originality---supports the existence of formality bias, but the effect magnitude from Qwen3-32B should not be directly compared with GPT-4o.\n15\t\n16\tThe NLI gate blocks \\emph{content} changes but cannot block quality perception shifts that are themselves mediated by style---a fundamental identifiability limit of any text-rewriting approach.\n17\t\n18\t\\section{Consistency Filter Robustness}\n19\t\\label{sec:consistency}\n20\t\n21\tThe position-consistency filter retains 55 of 98 valid pairs (56.1\\%); consistent and inconsistent groups show no significant differences across four covariates (NLI score $p = 0.824$, BERTScore $p = 0.12$, text length $p = 0.137$, length diff $p = 0.715$), indicating selection on judge decisiveness rather than text properties.\n22\tThe BT model using all 173 comparisons including inconsistent pairs yields 63.0\\% ($p = 0.0004$), consistent with the filtered sign test (73.9\\%, $p = 0.006$), confirming no systematic bias from the filter.\n23\t\n24\t\\section{GEE Model Diagnostics}\n25\t\\label{app:gee_diagnostics}\n26\t\n27\t\\paragraph{Correlation structure robustness.}\n28\tTable~\\ref{tab:gee_corr} compares exchangeable and independence working correlation structures across all six judge$\\times$axis cells.\n29\tThe two structures yield virtually identical results: $|\\Delta\\text{QIC}| < 0.001$ and maximum coefficient change ${<}0.02$ in every cell, with estimated within-pair correlations $\\hat{\\alpha} \\in [-0.25, +0.11]$.\n30\tAll GEE results reported in the main text are therefore robust to correlation structure specification.\n31\t\n32\t\\begin{table}[h]\n33\t\\centering\n34\t\\small\n35\t\\caption{GEE correlation structure comparison across six judge$\\times$axis cells.}\n36\t\\label{tab:gee_corr}\n37\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n38\t\\toprule\n39\tConfig & Exch.\\ QIC & Indep.\\ QIC & $|\\Delta\\text{QIC}|$ & Max$|\\Delta\\text{Coef}|$ \\\\\n40\t\\midrule\n41\tGPT-4o form.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n42\tQwen3 form.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n43\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n44\tQwen3 verb.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n45\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n46\tQwen3 reg.\\ & --- & --- & ${<}0.001$ & ${<}0.02$ \\\\\n47\t\\bottomrule\n48\t\\end{tabular}\n49\t\\end{table}\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Interaction test.}\n52\tTo verify that the additive GEE specification is appropriate, we test a style$\\times$originality interaction term on formality data for both judges.\n53\tThe interaction is non-significant in both cases (GPT-4o: $p = 0.53$; Qwen3-32B: $p = 0.32$), confirming that the additive model is the correct specification and that style and originality effects operate independently.\n54\t\n55\t\\paragraph{95\\% confidence intervals.}\n56\tTable~\\ref{tab:gee_ci} reports 95\\% CIs for all GEE odds ratios in Table~\\ref{tab:gee}.\n57\t\n58\t\\begin{table}[h]\n59\t\\centering\n60\t\\small\n61\t\\caption{95\\% CIs for GEE conditional decomposition (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).}\n62\t\\label{tab:gee_ci}\n63\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n64\t\\toprule\n65\tConfig & Style OR [CI] & Orig OR [CI] \\\\\n66\t\\midrule\n67\tQwen3 verb.\\ & 13.10 [5.62, 30.55] & 0.49 [0.21, 1.13] \\\\\n68\tGPT-4o verb.\\ & 11.34 & 0.45 \\\\\n69\tGPT-4o form.\\ & 2.58 & 2.32 \\\\\n70\tQwen3 form.\\ & 4.20 [1.99, 8.87] & 4.70 \\\\\n71\tQwen3 form.$^a$ & 2.15 [1.46, 3.16] & 4.99 [3.40, 7.34] \\\\\n72\tGPT-4o reg.\\ & 1.88 & 2.21 \\\\\n73\tQwen3 reg.\\ & 1.91 [1.01, 3.58] & 5.72 [3.02, 10.82] \\\\\n74\t\\bottomrule\n75\t\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\footnotesize $^a$ $n = 355$; other rows: $n = 88$.}\n76\t\\end{tabular}\n77\t\\end{table}\n78\t\n79\t\\section{Rewriter Independence}\n80\t\\label{app:rewriter_indep}\n81\t\n82\tTo test whether the Blindspot depends on the rewriter's identity, we replace Qwen3-32B with GPT-4o as rewriter while keeping Qwen3-32B as judge.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Verbosity (Table~\\ref{tab:rewriter_indep}).}\n85\tThe Style OR decreases by $22\\%$ ($13.10 \\to 10.17$), bounding evaluator-producer inflation of the style effect; the pure-style pattern (Originality n.s.) and Blindspot (BT significant, Likert n.s.) both persist.\n86\t\n87\t\\begin{table}[h]\n88\t\\centering\n89\t\\small\n90\t\\caption{Rewriter independence test: verbosity axis, Qwen3-32B judge. GPT-4o rewriter eliminates evaluator-producer overlap while preserving the Blindspot.}\n91\t\\label{tab:rewriter_indep}\n92\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n93\t\\toprule\n94\t & GPT-4o Rewriter & Qwen3 Rewriter \\\\\n95\t\\midrule\n96\tNLI pass rate & 81/100 (81\\%) & 86/100 (86\\%) \\\\\n97\tBT $P$ [95\\% CI] & .735 [.654, .809] & .773 [.703, .843] \\\\\n98\tLikert ATE ($p$) & .000 (.838) & ${\\sim}$0 (.179) \\\\\n99\tBlindspot & Yes & Yes \\\\\n100\tGEE Style OR ($p$) & 10.17 (${<}.0001$) & 13.10 (${<}.0001$) \\\\\n101\tGEE Orig OR ($p$) & 1.61 (n.s.) & 0.49 (n.s.) \\\\\n102\t\\bottomrule\n103\t\\end{tabular}\n104\t\\end{table}\n105\t\n106\t\\paragraph{Formality (Table~\\ref{tab:rewriter_indep_form}).}\n107\tGPT-4o rewriter produces formality-manipulated pairs yielding BT $64.4\\%$ $[56.1, 72.8]$, statistically indistinguishable from the Qwen3-32B baseline ($66.5\\%$ $[58.5, 73.9]$; $\\Delta = -2.1$pp).\n108\tThe Likert Blindspot replicates with the independent rewriter ($p = 0.184$, n.s.).\n109\t\n110\t\\begin{table}[h]\n111\t\\centering\n112\t\\small\n113\t\\caption{Rewriter independence test: formality axis, Qwen3-32B judge.}\n114\t\\label{tab:rewriter_indep_form}\n115\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n116\t\\toprule\n117\t & GPT-4o Rewriter & Qwen3 Rewriter \\\\\n118\t\\midrule\n119\tNLI pass rate & 90/100 (90\\%) & 88/100 (88\\%) \\\\\n120\tBT $P$ [95\\% CI] & .644 [.561, .728] & .665 [.585, .739] \\\\\n121\tLikert $d$ ($p$) & $-$.081 (.184) & .011 (.908) \\\\\n122\tBlindspot & Yes & Yes \\\\\n123\t\\bottomrule\n124\t\\end{tabular}\n125\t\\end{table}\n126\t\n127\t\\section{Direction-Aware Gate Validation}\n128\t\\label{app:gate_validation}\n129\t\n130\tFor verbosity, the direction-aware NLI gate admits 21 additional pairs (32\\%) compared to strict bidirectional verification.\n131\tThese pairs show consistent preference direction (BT 73.8\\% vs.\\ 78.5\\% for bidirectional-pass pairs), and overall BT estimates are statistically indistinguishable ($\\Delta = {-}1.1$pp, 95\\% CI $[{-}5.6, {+}3.0]$), confirming that direction-aware gating recovers valid pairs without inflating the effect.\n132\tFor formality, strict bidirectional verification is appropriate: a relaxed (direction-aware) gate would admit 9 additional pairs with reversed preference (BT 33.4\\%), indicating content preservation failure rather than genuine style preference.\n133\tThis asymmetry empirically justifies the axis-dependent gating strategy described in Section~\\ref{sec:nli_gate}.\n134\t\n135\t\\section{Claude Sonnet 4 Equivalence Testing}\n136\t\\label{app:claude_tost}\n137\t\n138\tWe failed to detect style bias for Claude at the current sample size ($n = 87$; BT $p = 0.091$, $p = 0.284$).\n139\tFormal equivalence testing \\citep[TOST;][]{lakens2017equivalence} at $\\Delta = 10$pp is underpowered at $n = 87$; at $\\Delta = 15$pp (corresponding to a small-to-medium effect in evaluation contexts) both axes confirm equivalence, bounding any Claude style preference to ${<}15$pp---substantively distinct from GPT-4o ($13$--$23$pp) and Qwen3-32B ($17$--$27$pp).\n140\t\n141\t\\section{Bradley-Terry Goodness of Fit and Stratified Analysis}\n142\t\\label{app:bt_gof}\n143\t\n144\tBradley-Terry goodness-of-fit tests confirm adequate model fit for both axes (formality: deviance $= 52.80$, $\\text{df} = 45$, $p = 0.198$; verbosity: $p = 0.905$).\n145\tStratified analysis of consistent pairs by original-text direction reveals no preference heterogeneity for verbosity (Fisher $p = 0.453$) but significant direction asymmetry for formality ($88.9\\%$ vs.\\ $52.6\\%$, Fisher $p = 0.015$), independently confirming the originality confound structure identified by GEE (Table~\\ref{tab:gee}).\n146\t\n147\tTo assess robustness to originality confounding, we restrict to pairs where the judge assigned equal Likert scores to original and counterfactual texts (eliminating detectable quality differences).\n148\tStyle preference persists: formality BT $= 67.3\\%$ ($n = 55$, $\\Delta = {+}0.8$pp from full sample, 95\\% CI $[{-}11.0, {+}12.6]$); verbosity BT $= 76.0\\%$ ($n = 50$, $\\Delta = {-}1.3$pp, 95\\% CI $[{-}13.3, {+}10.3]$).\n149\tIn the anti-originality subset (pairs where the original never wins), effects strengthen (formality $75.0\\%$, verbosity $86.1\\%$), confirming that style preference is not an artifact of originality confounding.\n150\t\n151\t\\section{Bradley-Terry Confidence Intervals}\n152\t\\label{app:bt_ci}\n153\t\n154\tTable~\\ref{tab:bt_ci} reports bootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals for all Bradley-Terry estimates in Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}.\n155\t\n156\t\\begin{table}[h]\n157\t\\centering\n158\t\\small\n159\t\\caption{Bootstrap 95\\% CIs for BT win probabilities (10{,}000 pair-level resamples).}\n160\t\\label{tab:bt_ci}\n161\t\\begin{tabular}{lllc}\n162\t\\toprule\n163\tBench & Judge & Axis & BT $P$ [95\\% CI] \\\\\n164\t\\midrule\n165\tLLMBar & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .630 [.557, .703] \\\\\n166\t & GPT-4o & Verb.\\ & .760 [.667, .853] \\\\\n167\t & GPT-4o & Reg.\\ & .570 [.468, .673] \\\\\n168\t\\cmidrule{2-4}\n169\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .665 [.585, .739] \\\\\n170\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .773 [.703, .843] \\\\\n171\t & Qwen3 & Reg.\\ & .553 [.479, .628] \\\\\n172\t\\cmidrule{2-4}\n173\t & Claude & Form.\\ & .562 [.489, .636] \\\\\n174\t & Claude & Verb.\\ & .541 [.459, .616] \\\\\n175\t\\midrule\n176\tAEval & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .727 [.663, .785] \\\\\n177\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .657 [.581, .733] \\\\\n178\t & Qwen3 & Verb.\\ & .841 [.774, .902] \\\\\n179\t\\midrule\n180\tMTB & GPT-4o & Form.\\ & .692 [.623, .753] \\\\\n181\t & Qwen3 & Form.\\ & .712 [.630, .788] \\\\\n182\t\\bottomrule\n183\t\\end{tabular}\n184\t\\end{table}\n185\t\n186\t\\section{Verbosity Sensitivity Hierarchy}\n187\t\\label{app:verb_sensitivity}\n188\t\n189\tTable~\\ref{tab:verb_sensitivity} replicates the seven-method sensitivity analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity}) on the verbosity axis ($n = 86$ NLI-filtered pairs, Qwen3-32B judge, LLMBar).\n190\tThe attenuation pattern is more extreme than formality: all six Likert-based methods remain non-significant (lowest $p = 0.213$) despite a stronger pairwise signal (BT 77.3\\% vs.\\ 66.5\\% for formality), and Likert effect sizes ($d = 0.036$--$0.056$) are near zero.\n191\t\n192\t\\begin{table}[h]\n193\t\\centering\n194\t\\small\n195\t\\caption{Sensitivity hierarchy for verbosity ($n = 86$, Qwen3-32B, LLMBar). All Likert methods fail; only BT reaches significance.}\n196\t\\label{tab:verb_sensitivity}\n197\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n198\t\\toprule\n199\tMethod & Statistic & $p$ & Sig? \\\\\n200\t\\midrule\n201\tBT model & 77.3\\% $[70.3, 84.3]$ & ${<}0.001$ & Yes \\\\\n202\t\\midrule\n203\tLikert ATE & $d = 0.056$ & 0.691 & No \\\\\n204\tBinarized $\\chi^2$ & OR $= 2.16$ & 0.213 & No \\\\\n205\tOrdinal reg.\\ & $\\beta = 0.159$ & 0.575 & No \\\\\n206\tPaired $t$-test & $d = 0.056$ & 0.607 & No \\\\\n207\tMann-Whitney $U$ & $r = {-}0.046$ & 0.578 & No \\\\\n208\tCohen's $d$ & $d = 0.036$ & --- & No \\\\\n209\t\\bottomrule\n210\t\\multicolumn{4}{p{0.9\\columnwidth}}{\\footnotesize Effect sizes use style-aligned scoring (verbose $-$ concise), isolating style preference direction. Table~\\ref{tab:main_results} reports RATE-compatible ATE (counterfactual $-$ original), which conflates style and originality effects.}\n211\t\\end{tabular}\n212\t\\end{table}\n213\t\n214\t\\section{NLI Gate Ablation}\n215\t\\label{app:nli_ablation}\n216\t\n217\tTable~\\ref{tab:nli_ablation} compares BT estimates across NLI-pass, NLI-fail, and all pairs.\n218\tFor formality, NLI-fail pairs reverse to 41.7\\% (preferring casual over formal), demonstrating that the gate removes content-confounded pairs rather than amplifying style signal.\n219\tThe small NLI-fail sample ($n \\approx 12$) limits precision; this serves as a bounding analysis.\n220\tFor verbosity, NLI-fail pairs remain at 75.0\\%, consistent with the dominant style effect (OR $= 13.10$) overwhelming content noise.\n221\t\n222\t\\begin{table}[h]\n223\t\\centering\n224\t\\small\n225\t\\caption{NLI gate ablation: BT win probability by NLI subset.}\n226\t\\label{tab:nli_ablation}\n227\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n228\t\\toprule\n229\tAxis & NLI-pass & NLI-fail & All \\\\\n230\t\\midrule\n231\tFormality & 66.5\\% & 41.7\\% & 63.0\\% \\\\\n232\tVerbosity & 77.3\\% & 75.0\\% & 76.0\\% \\\\\n233\t\\bottomrule\n234\t\\end{tabular}\n235\t\\end{table}\n236\t\n237\t\\section{Rewriting Artifact Control}\n238\t\\label{app:artifact_control}\n239\t\n240\tSame-style double-rewrite (DR) controls quantify the rewriting artifact: the proportion of pairs where the judge prefers the original over its same-style DR.\n241\tBoth judges systematically prefer LLM-polished text across axes (Table~\\ref{tab:artifact}).\n242\tFor verbosity, the near-zero original win rate indicates that rewriting quality dominates when content is held constant, validating the GEE decomposition that separates style from artifact effects.\n243\t\n244\t\\begin{table}[h]\n245\t\\centering\n246\t\\small\n247\t\\caption{Rewriting artifact: original win rate vs.\\ same-style double-rewrite.}\n248\t\\label{tab:artifact}\n249\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n250\t\\toprule\n251\tAxis & GPT-4o & Qwen3-32B \\\\\n252\t\\midrule\n253\tFormality & 31.8\\% ($p = .023$) & 26.7\\% ($p = .003$) \\\\\n254\tVerbosity & 6.2\\% ($p < .001$) & 0\\% ($p < .001$) \\\\\n255\t\\bottomrule\n256\t\\end{tabular}\n257\t\\end{table}\n258\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"87ac8a20-af64-4571-b3b4-98976bc1f23a","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:19:04.295Z"}
