{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Urkg7MXproS3QK1huPgJx9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: LLM Judge Style Bias — Research Landscape\n3\tdescription: 从10篇论文蒸馏的LLM-as-Judge风格偏差研究领域隐性经验：实验设计共识、方法趋势、竞品定位、研究空白\n4\ttype: research\n5\tsource_papers: 8 deep-read + 2 abstract-only (2018-2026)\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## 核心共识：Style 是 LLM Judge 的主宰偏差\n9\t\n10\tStyle-overall相关性R=0.999（SOS-Bench）；style bias 0.76-0.92远超position bias≤0.04（Soumik 2026）；style/format是preference leakage主载体（Preference Leakage ICLR'26）。**支持度：5/8，置信度：高。**\n11\t\n12\t## 标准实验设计模式\n13\t\n14\t**Judge模型覆盖**：至少3-5个异构LLM（GPT-4o + Claude + Gemini/Llama + 开源），跨provider验证是reviewer期待的最低标准。支持度：5/8，置信度：高（LC-AlpacaEval, SOS-Bench, Preference Leakage, Soumik, FairJudge）。\n15\t\n16\t**控制干预实验**：注入已知缺陷（事实错误/风格变化/特征抑制）量化judge敏感度差异，比纯相关性分析更有因果说服力。支持度：4/8，置信度：高（SOS-Bench的sarcasm/-96% vs wrong/-13%，CASSE的12×12矩阵，Soumik的controlled dataset）。\n17\t\n18\t**统计严谨性标配**：Bootstrap 95% CIs（n≥2000）+ McNemar/显著性检验 + Holm-Bonferroni多重比较校正。支持度：3/8，置信度：中（Soumik, Preference Leakage, RATE）。\n19\t\n20\t**半合成验证**：在已知ground truth的合成数据上先验证，再推广到真实数据。支持度：2/8，置信度：中（RATE的typo实验, Veitch的semi-synthetic peer review）。\n21\t\n22\t## 标准Benchmark与指标\n23\t\n24\t**Benchmark频率**：AlpacaEval/Arena-Hard（各2/8）、MT-Bench（2/8）、Chatbot Arena作为人类参考（2/8）。自建controlled dataset成为趋势（SOS-Bench 152K, Soumik 225, CASSE 12200）。\n25\t\n26\t**指标**：Win Rate（5/8）、Spearman Correlation with human（3/8）、Human Agreement（3/8）、Bias Score（2/8）、Cohen's kappa（2/8）。因果方向：ATE/Cohen's d（RATE）。\n27\t\n28\t## 方法论趋势\n29\t\n30\t**上升**：因果推断框架用于judge debiasing（LC-AlpacaEval用causal mediation，RATE用double-rewrite ATE，Counterfactual Rhetorical用Bradley-Terry）；LLM生成反事实文本作为干预工具；多维度交叉偏差分析。\n31\t\n32\t**消退**：单维度偏差分析（仅length或仅position）；无统计检验的裸数字比较；纯prompt工程debiasing（Soumik证明效果model-dependent）。\n33\t\n34\t## 竞品Claim与定位\n35\t\n36\t| 关系 | 论文 | 核心Claim |\n37\t|------|------|-----------|\n38\t| Competitor | Preference Leakage (ICLR'26) | 模型家族关系通过style泄漏偏好，contextual calibration降59%Error Bias |\n39\t| Competitor | Soumik (2026) | CoT是唯一universally safe策略，debiasing效果model-dependent |\n40\t| Competitor | FairJudge (2026) | 训练侧三阶段(SFT→DPO→GRPO)，8B超72B/671B |\n41\t| Foundation | LC-AlpacaEval (ICLR'25) | Length as causal mediator，回归去偏 |\n42\t| Foundation | SOS-Bench (ICLR'25) | Style-overall R=0.999，sarcasm/-96% vs wrong/-13% |\n43\t| Foundation | RATE (ICML'25) | Double-rewrite消除反事实系统误差 |\n44\t| Foundation | Counterfactual Rhetorical | Persona反事实+BT聚合度量修辞风格 |\n45\t| Complementary | CASSE (2026) | Style特征因果纠缠，mitigation面临alignment tax |\n46\t\n47\t## 研究空白（我们的机会）\n48\t\n49\t1. **多维Style因果分解**：现有工作要么把style当单一维度(length/整体style)，要么只做两两交互(CASSE)，无人做judge评分中多维style的联合因果分解。支持度：交叉盲区，置信度：高。\n50\t2. **Style bias × Preference leakage联合处理**：两类偏差被独立研究，无人考虑交互。置信度：中。\n51\t3. **Debiasing的下游对齐影响**：所有论文止步于\"judge更准了\"，无人验证去偏judge是否改善实际模型对齐。置信度：中。\n52\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"b7881308-ad2a-480f-b797-b084a28aca9e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:19:09.647Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a049eb25ffdd70ed8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LygPnDBjmQXz9UNmYayUzR","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":62388,"tool_uses":20,"duration_ms":132218},"uuid":"b9e5422e-a65c-47ad-9ba8-e98ba7f96ba4","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LygPnDBjmQXz9UNmYayUzR","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n**Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Focus**\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper introduces Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box framework for detecting style bias in LLM judges by combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style rewriting, and position-controlled pairwise comparison with Bradley-Terry modeling. The central empirical finding is the \"Likert Blindspot\": pairwise comparison detects statistically significant style bias (BT 63-84%) in conditions where Likert scoring yields non-significant results, even at n=355. GEE decomposition reveals a two-tier confound structure (verbosity = pure style; formality = mixed style + originality). Experiments span 2 target judges + 1 negative control, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-operationalized measurement phenomenon.** The Likert Blindspot is not merely asserted but systematically demonstrated through a 7-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2/sensitivity table), a scaled replication at n=355 that rules out power as the explanation (d converges to ~0 rather than staying positive), and a power-curve analysis grounded in observed effect sizes rather than post-hoc power. This level of measurement rigor is above the median for *ACL papers.\n\n- **GEE confound decomposition is a genuine methodological contribution.** The per-axis stratification into pure style (verbosity: Style OR 11-13, Originality NS), mixed (formality: both significant), and originality-dominated (register) is informative and honest. The interaction test (p=0.53, 0.32) and correlation structure robustness (exchangeable vs. independence DQIC <0.001) are correctly reported. Few NLP papers specify GEE diagnostics at this level.\n\n- **Multiple layers of confound control, honestly reported.** The paper does not sweep rewriting artifacts under the rug. The double-rewrite placebo (Appendix Table 8) quantifies that judges prefer polished text (formality: 68-73% DR win; verbosity: 94-100%), and the GEE originality term is positioned as the statistical control for this artifact. The NLI gate ablation (formality BT reversal to 41.7% on NLI-fail pairs) is the single strongest piece of evidence against the amplification confound. Claude negative control (BT 54-56%, NS on both axes) provides discriminative validity.\n\n- **Rewriter independence is validated on both axes.** GPT-4o as alternative rewriter produces BT 64.4%/73.5% vs. Qwen3-32B baseline 66.5%/77.3% with overlapping CIs. This addresses evaluator-producer bias directly.\n\n- **Statistical framework is mostly sound.** Holm-Bonferroni correction on the primary 2x3 family, clear separation of confirmatory vs. exploratory analyses, bootstrap CIs with pair-level resampling, and BT goodness-of-fit tests are all appropriate.\n\n- **Honest limitations section.** The paper acknowledges the quality-style identifiability limit, the evaluator-producer overlap, and the alternative construct-difference interpretation of the Blindspot.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) The amplification-vs-detection question is not resolved, only bounded.** The Comparative Trap (Jeong et al. 2025) is the most serious threat to the paper's claims. The paper offers four pieces of evidence (NLI ablation, Claude control, cross-axis gradient, debiased prompt), but none individually or collectively rules out partial amplification. Specifically:\n  - The NLI ablation reversal (41.7% on formality NLI-fail) shows the gate cleans content noise, but does not address whether the pairwise FORMAT itself amplifies real-but-tiny preferences into the 63-77% range.\n  - Claude showing no bias only proves that the format does not fabricate bias from nothing; it does not prove the format does not amplify existing micro-preferences in GPT-4o/Qwen3.\n  - The cross-axis gradient (55%/67%/77%) is consistent with either detection-of-varying-true-effects or amplification-of-varying-micro-effects.\n  - The paper correctly concedes this (line \"CSD cannot fully separate detection from amplification of micro-preferences\") but the abstract and introduction frame the finding as \"detecting hidden style bias\" rather than \"detecting-or-amplifying,\" which overstates the contribution. **Severity: major, affects C1/C2 framing.**\n\n- **(Major) Formality amplification fraction is concerning and underdiscussed.** From the placebo analysis: formality net effect is 7.5-11.7pp above 50%, with amplification fraction 29-42%. This means that up to 42% of the formality signal could be rewriting artifact. The paper relies on GEE to decompose this, but the GEE Style OR for formality (2.58 for GPT-4o) has a wide implicit CI and the originality confound IS significant (OR=2.32, p=0.006). The formality claim should be further downgraded from \"conditional style effect\" to \"style effect with substantial residual confounding that GEE may not fully resolve.\" Currently the paper treats formality as a confirmed (if conditional) bias; the evidence supports at best a marginal finding on this axis. **Severity: major.**\n\n- **(Major) Only 2 target judges in the submitted version.** The abstract says \"two target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) and one negative control (Claude Sonnet 4).\" The field consensus (per multiple concurrent papers: SOS-Bench, Preference Leakage, Soumik 2026) is 3-5 heterogeneous judges minimum. Having Gemini and Llama mentioned in limitations as \"needed\" but absent weakens generalizability claims substantially. Two judges from two different providers is the bare minimum; three would make the story much more convincing. The Qwen3-32B evaluator-producer overlap further reduces effective independence to ~1.5 truly independent judge-rewriter configurations. **Severity: major, directly affects acceptance threshold.**\n\n- **(Minor) Base sample size n=88 is small for GEE with two covariates.** GEE with exchangeable correlation on n=88 clusters (176 observations) with two binary predictors is technically feasible but has limited power for detecting interaction effects or for the OR estimates to be precise. The verbosity CIs (Style OR 13.10 [5.62, 30.55]) span a 5.4x range. For formality, the n=355 replication is reassuring (Style OR drops from 4.20 to 2.15), but the n=88 GPT-4o formality Style OR (2.58) has no reported CI in the main table. The appendix partially addresses this but many cells are missing CIs. **Severity: minor-to-moderate.**\n\n- **(Minor) The BT threshold claim for Likert detection rests on very few data points.** The attenuation spectrum is a compelling narrative, but the \"BT>70% for partial Likert detection\" threshold is derived from ~3 exceptions (MT-Bench formality for both judges + AlpacaEval verbosity). With only 3 data points above the threshold and 6 below, this is pattern-recognition from a small grid rather than a statistically grounded claim. The paper should acknowledge this more explicitly. **Severity: minor.**\n\n- **(Minor) NLI gate validation uses LLM annotator, not humans.** The gate validation relies on Qwen3-32B as annotator (the same model used as rewriter and judge in some configurations). GPT-4o cross-validation on all 200 pairs is reported (92.1%/82.6%) but this is still LLM-vs-LLM. No human evaluation of content preservation is presented. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but the lack weakens the content-preservation claim that is foundational to the entire pipeline. **Severity: minor but structural.**\n\n- **(Minor) Register axis adds noise more than signal.** Register BT is 55-57% with marginal p-values (0.04 for GPT-4o, 0.076 for Qwen3-32B), excluded from the Holm family, and GEE shows originality dominates (OR=5.72 vs. Style OR=1.91). Including it in the paper risks diluting the stronger formality/verbosity findings. The paper does label it \"exploratory\" but it occupies non-trivial text real estate. **Severity: minor.**\n\n- **(Minor) The DAG (Figure 1) claims interventional identification but the text correctly walks this back.** The figure shows blocked R->Q and R->Y paths, but the placebo analysis confirms R->Y is NOT blocked (judges prefer polished text). The dashed orange S->Q path is acknowledged but the visual impression of the DAG overpromises relative to the actual identification achieved. **Severity: minor, presentation issue.**\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide a quantitative upper bound on how much of the observed BT signal could be format-level amplification (as distinct from rewriting artifact)? The Claude control bounds fabrication-from-nothing, but what bounds amplification-of-existing-small-preferences?\n\n2. The formality GEE Style OR drops from 4.20 to 2.15 when n increases from 88 to 355. Is this purely a small-sample correction, or could it indicate that the n=88 estimates for other cells (verbosity, register) are similarly inflated? Have you computed the expected shrinkage?\n\n3. For the NLI gate ablation, the NLI-fail formality sample is n~12. What is the exact n, and what is the 95% CI on that 41.7% BT estimate? The reversal is suggestive but underpowered.\n\n4. The verbosity placebo shows 0-6% original win rate, meaning judges overwhelmingly prefer ANY rewrite over original text for verbosity. Doesn't this mean the GEE \"originality\" term for verbosity is capturing something extreme (artifact >> 0) that then partially cancels the style term, potentially deflating the Style OR? The claimed OR=13.10 as \"clean\" may be entangled with this cancellation.\n\n5. Why is Gemini-2.0-Flash mentioned in the abstract context but absent from all tables? If API access was available (the memory mentions it), what prevented its inclusion?\n\n6. The scaled replication (n=355) exists only for Qwen3-32B formality. Is there a principled reason not to scale up the strongest finding (verbosity) where the claims are most ambitious?\n\n### Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5 |\n| **Excitement** | 3.0 |\n| **Reproducibility** | 3.5 |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 3.0 (Findings) |\n| **Confidence** | 4 (High) |\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper identifies a real measurement phenomenon (Likert attenuation for LLM judge style bias) and applies appropriate statistical methodology. The GEE decomposition, rewriter independence checks, NLI ablation, and Claude negative control collectively represent above-average experimental rigor. However, three major issues prevent a Main recommendation: (1) the detection-vs-amplification question remains open and the framing leans toward \"detection\" without sufficient warrant; (2) formality findings have substantial residual confounding that is acknowledged but not reflected in the claim strength; (3) the judge coverage (effectively 2 target judges with evaluator-producer overlap on one) falls below field expectations. The novelty ceiling is also a factor: the individual components (NLI gating, BT modeling, GEE, pairwise comparison) are all established tools; the contribution is integrative. For Findings, the measurement phenomenon is well-documented and practically relevant. Adding a third independent target judge, tightening the amplification bounds, and softening formality claims would strengthen the paper toward Main."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a049eb25ffdd70ed8 (use SendMessage with to: 'a049eb25ffdd70ed8' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 64625\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 132222</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"dfa9d073-3043-4a57-9ccf-d4045ce0581f","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:20:18.196Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. \n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1-5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5). Main acceptance ~21%.\n\n## Paper: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n### Abstract\nLLM-as-judge evaluation relies on style-biased judges, but standard Likert evaluation fails to detect this. The paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a black-box framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style manipulation, and position-controlled pairwise comparison. Tested on 5 judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Gemini-2.0-Flash, Llama-3.3-70B, Claude Sonnet 4), 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), 3 style axes (verbosity, formality, register). Main finding: \"Likert Blindspot\" — pairwise BT detects significant style bias (63-77%) where Likert yields NS results. Placebo controls bound rewriting artifacts to ~5-7pp vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\nCSD has 5 components per style axis:\n1. **Counterfactual Generation**: Rewrite responses along a single style axis using Qwen3-32B (primary), GPT-4o, Llama-3.1-70B as independence controls.\n2. **NLI-Gated Content Control**: Bidirectional NLI filtering with τ=0.90. Pairs failing gate excluded (~10-15% rejection).\n3. **Position-Controlled Pairwise Comparison**: Both orderings per pair; \"preferred\" only when consistent across both orderings.\n4. **Bradley-Terry Modeling**: BT model on pairwise data, 95% bootstrap CIs, likelihood ratio test for H0: p_style=0.50.\n5. **GEE Confound Decomposition**: GEE logistic model with style and originality covariates to separate style from content-quality confounds.\n\n### Results\n**Table 1 (LLMBar main results):**\n- Verbosity: GPT-4o BT=76.0% (p<.001), Qwen3=77.3% (p<.001), Llama=66.0% (p=.002). All Likert NS. Claude=54.1% NS (negative control). 3/3 Blindspot.\n- Formality: GPT-4o=63.0% (p<.001), Qwen3=66.5% (p<.001), Gemini=61.5% (p=.004), Llama=54.8% (p=.18 NS), Claude=56.2% NS. 3/4 Blindspot (excl Llama).\n\n**Table 2 (Cross-benchmark formality BT):**\n- Formality replicates across LLMBar/AlpacaEval/MT-Bench for GPT-4o (63-72.7%) and Qwen3 (66.5-71.2%). Claude NS everywhere. 5/6 cells Blindspot; MT-Bench Qwen3 Likert p=.022 is exception.\n\n**Table 3 (GEE decomposition):**\n- Verbosity: style ORs 5.21-13.10 (all p<.001), originality ORs all NS → pure style effect\n- Formality: both style and originality ORs significant for GPT-4o/Qwen3/Gemini; Llama only originality sig → conditional effect\n- Register: style ORs marginal, originality ORs dominant\n\n**Table 4 (Attenuation spectrum):**\n- BT 55-67%: 0/8 Likert detection\n- BT 68-77%: 1/6 Likert detection (17%)\n- BT >80%: 1/1 Likert detection (100%)\n\n**Table 5 (Amplification bounds):**\n- Placebo BT 54.8-57.1% (~5-7pp above 50%)\n- Verbosity net effect ~20pp, amplification fraction 24-26%\n- Formality net effect 7.5-11.7pp, amplification fraction 29-42%\n\n**Table 6 (Robustness):**\n- Rewriter independence: 3 rewriters produce BT 63.8-66.5% with overlapping CIs\n- NLI ablation: NLI-pass BT slightly higher but difference NS (small NLI-fail n=12-14)\n\n**Discussion mentions:** prompt-level debiasing is insufficient (tested \"style-blind\" prompts, Qwen3 -5.7pp still sig, GPT-4o -0.4pp negligible).\n\n### Imagined experiments from review context (check coverage):\n- exp-001 (Gemini formality): ✓ covered in Table 1\n- exp-002 (cross-benchmark formality): ✓ covered in Table 2\n- exp-003 (sensitivity analysis): ✓ covered in Table 4\n- exp-005 (double-rewrite control): ✓ covered in Table 5\n- exp-009 (Llama rewriter): ✓ covered in Table 6\n- exp-021 (Llama judge): ✓ covered in Table 1\n- exp-022 (placebo BT): ✓ covered in Table 5\n- exp-023 (NLI ablation): ✓ covered in Table 6\n- exp-024 (cross-axis gradient): ✓ covered in Section 5.6\n\nAll imagined experiments are covered in the paper.\n\n### Your task\nWrite a detailed R1 review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the CSD pipeline well-designed? Are the statistical methods (BT, GEE, bootstrap) appropriate? Any methodological flaws?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are controls adequate? Is the sample size sufficient? Are claims well-supported by evidence?\n3. **Specific concerns**: Amplification confound (especially formality at 42%), NLI gate design choices, BT threshold claim (only 1 cell >80%), GEE model specification.\n4. **Reproducibility**: Can this be replicated? Are enough details provided?\n\nOutput format — produce a structured review:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, specific)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, half-step)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards (~21% Main acceptance).","agentId":"a049eb25ffdd70ed8","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n**Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments Focus**\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper introduces Controlled Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box framework for detecting style bias in LLM judges by combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style rewriting, and position-controlled pairwise comparison with Bradley-Terry modeling. The central empirical finding is the \"Likert Blindspot\": pairwise comparison detects statistically significant style bias (BT 63-84%) in conditions where Likert scoring yields non-significant results, even at n=355. GEE decomposition reveals a two-tier confound structure (verbosity = pure style; formality = mixed style + originality). Experiments span 2 target judges + 1 negative control, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-operationalized measurement phenomenon.** The Likert Blindspot is not merely asserted but systematically demonstrated through a 7-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2/sensitivity table), a scaled replication at n=355 that rules out power as the explanation (d converges to ~0 rather than staying positive), and a power-curve analysis grounded in observed effect sizes rather than post-hoc power. This level of measurement rigor is above the median for *ACL papers.\n\n- **GEE confound decomposition is a genuine methodological contribution.** The per-axis stratification into pure style (verbosity: Style OR 11-13, Originality NS), mixed (formality: both significant), and originality-dominated (register) is informative and honest. The interaction test (p=0.53, 0.32) and correlation structure robustness (exchangeable vs. independence DQIC <0.001) are correctly reported. Few NLP papers specify GEE diagnostics at this level.\n\n- **Multiple layers of confound control, honestly reported.** The paper does not sweep rewriting artifacts under the rug. The double-rewrite placebo (Appendix Table 8) quantifies that judges prefer polished text (formality: 68-73% DR win; verbosity: 94-100%), and the GEE originality term is positioned as the statistical control for this artifact. The NLI gate ablation (formality BT reversal to 41.7% on NLI-fail pairs) is the single strongest piece of evidence against the amplification confound. Claude negative control (BT 54-56%, NS on both axes) provides discriminative validity.\n\n- **Rewriter independence is validated on both axes.** GPT-4o as alternative rewriter produces BT 64.4%/73.5% vs. Qwen3-32B baseline 66.5%/77.3% with overlapping CIs. This addresses evaluator-producer bias directly.\n\n- **Statistical framework is mostly sound.** Holm-Bonferroni correction on the primary 2x3 family, clear separation of confirmatory vs. exploratory analyses, bootstrap CIs with pair-level resampling, and BT goodness-of-fit tests are all appropriate.\n\n- **Honest limitations section.** The paper acknowledges the quality-style identifiability limit, the evaluator-producer overlap, and the alternative construct-difference interpretation of the Blindspot.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) The amplification-vs-detection question is not resolved, only bounded.** The Comparative Trap (Jeong et al. 2025) is the most serious threat to the paper's claims. The paper offers four pieces of evidence (NLI ablation, Claude control, cross-axis gradient, debiased prompt), but none individually or collectively rules out partial amplification. Specifically:\n  - The NLI ablation reversal (41.7% on formality NLI-fail) shows the gate cleans content noise, but does not address whether the pairwise FORMAT itself amplifies real-but-tiny preferences into the 63-77% range.\n  - Claude showing no bias only proves that the format does not fabricate bias from nothing; it does not prove the format does not amplify existing micro-preferences in GPT-4o/Qwen3.\n  - The cross-axis gradient (55%/67%/77%) is consistent with either detection-of-varying-true-effects or amplification-of-varying-micro-effects.\n  - The paper correctly concedes this (line \"CSD cannot fully separate detection from amplification of micro-preferences\") but the abstract and introduction frame the finding as \"detecting hidden style bias\" rather than \"detecting-or-amplifying,\" which overstates the contribution. **Severity: major, affects C1/C2 framing.**\n\n- **(Major) Formality amplification fraction is concerning and underdiscussed.** From the placebo analysis: formality net effect is 7.5-11.7pp above 50%, with amplification fraction 29-42%. This means that up to 42% of the formality signal could be rewriting artifact. The paper relies on GEE to decompose this, but the GEE Style OR for formality (2.58 for GPT-4o) has a wide implicit CI and the originality confound IS significant (OR=2.32, p=0.006). The formality claim should be further downgraded from \"conditional style effect\" to \"style effect with substantial residual confounding that GEE may not fully resolve.\" Currently the paper treats formality as a confirmed (if conditional) bias; the evidence supports at best a marginal finding on this axis. **Severity: major.**\n\n- **(Major) Only 2 target judges in the submitted version.** The abstract says \"two target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) and one negative control (Claude Sonnet 4).\" The field consensus (per multiple concurrent papers: SOS-Bench, Preference Leakage, Soumik 2026) is 3-5 heterogeneous judges minimum. Having Gemini and Llama mentioned in limitations as \"needed\" but absent weakens generalizability claims substantially. Two judges from two different providers is the bare minimum; three would make the story much more convincing. The Qwen3-32B evaluator-producer overlap further reduces effective independence to ~1.5 truly independent judge-rewriter configurations. **Severity: major, directly affects acceptance threshold.**\n\n- **(Minor) Base sample size n=88 is small for GEE with two covariates.** GEE with exchangeable correlation on n=88 clusters (176 observations) with two binary predictors is technically feasible but has limited power for detecting interaction effects or for the OR estimates to be precise. The verbosity CIs (Style OR 13.10 [5.62, 30.55]) span a 5.4x range. For formality, the n=355 replication is reassuring (Style OR drops from 4.20 to 2.15), but the n=88 GPT-4o formality Style OR (2.58) has no reported CI in the main table. The appendix partially addresses this but many cells are missing CIs. **Severity: minor-to-moderate.**\n\n- **(Minor) The BT threshold claim for Likert detection rests on very few data points.** The attenuation spectrum is a compelling narrative, but the \"BT>70% for partial Likert detection\" threshold is derived from ~3 exceptions (MT-Bench formality for both judges + AlpacaEval verbosity). With only 3 data points above the threshold and 6 below, this is pattern-recognition from a small grid rather than a statistically grounded claim. The paper should acknowledge this more explicitly. **Severity: minor.**\n\n- **(Minor) NLI gate validation uses LLM annotator, not humans.** The gate validation relies on Qwen3-32B as annotator (the same model used as rewriter and judge in some configurations). GPT-4o cross-validation on all 200 pairs is reported (92.1%/82.6%) but this is still LLM-vs-LLM. No human evaluation of content preservation is presented. The paper acknowledges this in limitations but the lack weakens the content-preservation claim that is foundational to the entire pipeline. **Severity: minor but structural.**\n\n- **(Minor) Register axis adds noise more than signal.** Register BT is 55-57% with marginal p-values (0.04 for GPT-4o, 0.076 for Qwen3-32B), excluded from the Holm family, and GEE shows originality dominates (OR=5.72 vs. Style OR=1.91). Including it in the paper risks diluting the stronger formality/verbosity findings. The paper does label it \"exploratory\" but it occupies non-trivial text real estate. **Severity: minor.**\n\n- **(Minor) The DAG (Figure 1) claims interventional identification but the text correctly walks this back.** The figure shows blocked R->Q and R->Y paths, but the placebo analysis confirms R->Y is NOT blocked (judges prefer polished text). The dashed orange S->Q path is acknowledged but the visual impression of the DAG overpromises relative to the actual identification achieved. **Severity: minor, presentation issue.**\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide a quantitative upper bound on how much of the observed BT signal could be format-level amplification (as distinct from rewriting artifact)? The Claude control bounds fabrication-from-nothing, but what bounds amplification-of-existing-small-preferences?\n\n2. The formality GEE Style OR drops from 4.20 to 2.15 when n increases from 88 to 355. Is this purely a small-sample correction, or could it indicate that the n=88 estimates for other cells (verbosity, register) are similarly inflated? Have you computed the expected shrinkage?\n\n3. For the NLI gate ablation, the NLI-fail formality sample is n~12. What is the exact n, and what is the 95% CI on that 41.7% BT estimate? The reversal is suggestive but underpowered.\n\n4. The verbosity placebo shows 0-6% original win rate, meaning judges overwhelmingly prefer ANY rewrite over original text for verbosity. Doesn't this mean the GEE \"originality\" term for verbosity is capturing something extreme (artifact >> 0) that then partially cancels the style term, potentially deflating the Style OR? The claimed OR=13.10 as \"clean\" may be entangled with this cancellation.\n\n5. Why is Gemini-2.0-Flash mentioned in the abstract context but absent from all tables? If API access was available (the memory mentions it), what prevented its inclusion?\n\n6. The scaled replication (n=355) exists only for Qwen3-32B formality. Is there a principled reason not to scale up the strongest finding (verbosity) where the claims are most ambitious?\n\n### Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5 |\n| **Excitement** | 3.0 |\n| **Reproducibility** | 3.5 |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 3.0 (Findings) |\n| **Confidence** | 4 (High) |\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper identifies a real measurement phenomenon (Likert attenuation for LLM judge style bias) and applies appropriate statistical methodology. The GEE decomposition, rewriter independence checks, NLI ablation, and Claude negative control collectively represent above-average experimental rigor. However, three major issues prevent a Main recommendation: (1) the detection-vs-amplification question remains open and the framing leans toward \"detection\" without sufficient warrant; (2) formality findings have substantial residual confounding that is acknowledged but not reflected in the claim strength; (3) the judge coverage (effectively 2 target judges with evaluator-producer overlap on one) falls below field expectations. The novelty ceiling is also a factor: the individual components (NLI gating, BT modeling, GEE, pairwise comparison) are all established tools; the contribution is integrative. For Findings, the measurement phenomenon is well-documented and practically relevant. Adding a third independent target judge, tightening the amplification bounds, and softening formality claims would strengthen the paper toward Main."}],"totalDurationMs":132222,"totalTokens":64625,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1719,"cache_read_input_tokens":59994,"output_tokens":2911,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1719},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2911,"cache_read_input_tokens":59994,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1719,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1719,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a12130b52692934ee","tool_use_id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":48846,"tool_uses":16,"duration_ms":123159},"uuid":"a99eb71c-48cb-4d86-a59c-d4b46826ed76","session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018FLttGYH1EnWqWoR1q5pAr","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its positioning, the competitive landscape, and the project's internal assessment. Let me now write the structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 (R2) — Focus: Novelty and Related Work Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a black-box framework that combines NLI-gated content control, per-axis style rewriting, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect style bias in LLM judges. The central empirical finding -- the \"Likert Blindspot\" -- is that pairwise comparison detects style biases (BT 63-77%) that Likert scoring completely misses (all p > 0.17), and that this is measurement attenuation rather than a power gap (confirmed at n=355). GEE decomposition reveals axis-specific confound structures: verbosity is a clean style effect, formality is conditional on originality, and register is originality-dominated.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-executed measurement methodology comparison.** The seven-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2) is a convincing demonstration. Applying seven distinct statistical approaches to identical data and showing that all five Likert-based methods fail while both pairwise methods succeed is rigorous. The scaled replication at n=355 distinguishing attenuation from power is particularly well-designed -- this is the single strongest piece of evidence in the paper.\n\n- **Honest treatment of confounds and limitations.** The paper does not oversell. The authors acknowledge the rewriting artifact (Table 7 shows judges prefer polished text at 68-100% rates), disclose evaluator-producer bias with Qwen3-32B, present the GEE decomposition as partial rather than complete deconfounding, and honestly discuss the Comparative Trap concern. The dashed orange S-to-Q path in the DAG and the explicit \"not a claim of perfect identification\" framing is commendable. This level of intellectual honesty is rare.\n\n- **GEE confound decomposition reveals useful structure.** The two-tier finding -- verbosity as pure style effect (OR 11-13, Orig NS) vs. formality as conditional (both OR significant) vs. register as originality-dominated -- is a genuinely useful empirical contribution. This per-axis confound gradient is novel and actionable for practitioners designing evaluation pipelines.\n\n- **NLI gate ablation provides informative evidence against amplification.** The formality NLI-fail reversal to BT 41.7% is a clever natural experiment. If pairwise comparison merely amplified any existing signal, removing the content gate should amplify further; instead it reverses. This does not fully resolve the Comparative Trap concern, but it is a meaningful bound.\n\n- **Statistical rigor throughout.** Holm-Bonferroni correction on the primary family, clear separation of confirmatory vs. exploratory analyses, BT goodness-of-fit tests, GEE correlation structure robustness checks, and honest reporting of the exploratory status of register results. The paper meets or exceeds the statistical standards of the subfield.\n\n- **Practical writing quality.** The paper is well-organized, the DAG is clear, the tables are readable, and the claims are appropriately hedged. The progression from method to results to analysis is logical.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) The \"Likert Blindspot\" repackages a well-known psychometric principle as a novel discovery.** Thurstone (1927) established that pairwise comparison yields finer discriminability than absolute rating nearly a century ago. DeCoster et al. (2011), cited in the paper, show that categorization of continuous variables reduces power. Preston & Colman (2000), also cited, demonstrate that scale granularity affects reliability. The paper's own Related Work (Section 2) acknowledges this lineage. The specific contribution is quantifying this gap for LLM judges on style bias -- but this is domain application, not methodological novelty. The \"Blindspot\" framing implies a surprising, hidden phenomenon; in reality it is a predicted consequence of measurement theory applied to a new domain. The paper would be more honest (and arguably stronger) if it framed this as \"confirming and quantifying a theoretically predicted measurement limitation\" rather than \"detecting a hidden blindspot.\" The catchy name risks overclaiming.\n\n- **(Major) Narrow judge coverage undermines generalizability claims.** The primary experiments use only 2 target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) plus one negative control (Claude). The paper's own internal notes target 3-5 heterogeneous judges as the reviewer-expected minimum. Llama-3.3-70B is listed as \"pending\" but not included. Gemini is mentioned in the abstract/framing but absent from results. Two target judges from different providers is the absolute minimum; the absence of a third open-source judge (Llama, Mistral) with complete results weakens the claim that findings generalize. The Qwen3-32B dual role as rewriter and judge, while bounded by the rewriter independence test, remains an uncomfortable confound.\n\n- **(Major) The primary benchmark is extremely small (n=100/88 after NLI filtering).** LLMBar has only 100 samples. After NLI filtering, the effective n drops to 86-88 for most conditions. While the n=355 scaled replication for one condition (Qwen3 formality) addresses power concerns for that specific cell, most results rest on ~88 pairs. The BT confidence intervals are wide (e.g., GPT-4o formality: [0.557, 0.703]). The register axis at n=88 yields marginal significance (p=0.04, p=0.076) that would not survive Holm correction -- and to the authors' credit, they acknowledge this. But the overall evidential weight is limited by scale. Cross-benchmark results (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) are provided only for formality and one verbosity condition, not the full grid.\n\n- **(Major) Relationship to RATE is positioned unfairly.** The paper claims RATE's Likert ATE is a \"vulnerability\" and implies RATE's findings \"may underestimate the true magnitude of attribute effects.\" But RATE explicitly uses a double-rewrite correction protocol to handle imperfect counterfactuals -- a protocol CSD itself adopts (Section 3.1). CSD's claim that Likert is structurally insensitive is interesting, but the framing implies RATE's results are invalid, when in fact RATE and CSD answer different questions. RATE asks \"what is the causal ATE on a reward model's score?\" (an absolute quantity); CSD asks \"can we detect ordinal preference?\" (a relative comparison). These are different estimands, and one being more sensitive does not invalidate the other. The paper should acknowledge this distinction more carefully.\n\n- **(Minor) Missing comparison with Counterfactual Rhetorical (Salinas et al., 2024).** The paper notes this work uses a \"similar pipeline\" (LLM counterfactual rewrite + BT aggregation) but dismisses it as operating at \"persona-level granularity.\" This understates the overlap. Both papers use LLM-generated counterfactuals, NLI or similar content verification, and Bradley-Terry modeling. The key differentiator -- per-axis vs. persona-level granularity, and the Likert comparison -- should be articulated more precisely with empirical rather than just conceptual differentiation.\n\n- **(Minor) No direct empirical comparison with any baseline method.** CSD is positioned against RATE, LC-AlpacaEval, CASSE, and Counterfactual Rhetorical, but no experiment runs any of these methods on the same data for head-to-head comparison. The RATE comparison is the closest (applying RATE's Likert ATE to CSD's data), but even this is a CSD-controlled comparison, not running RATE's full pipeline. Running LC-AlpacaEval's regression debiasing or RATE's double-rewrite correction on the same benchmark would substantially strengthen the comparative claims.\n\n- **(Minor) The placebo control reveals a substantial rewriting artifact that complicates interpretation.** Table 7 shows judges prefer double-rewrites over originals at 68-100% rates. The paper argues GEE controls for this, but the magnitude of the artifact (verbosity: 94-100% prefer DR) is striking. This means the \"original vs. style-changed\" comparison confounds style change with rewriting polish. While GEE attempts to separate these, the Originality OR for formality is significant (2.32, p=0.006), meaning the decomposition is only partial. The verbosity clean result is reassuring, but formality -- the axis with the most nuanced claims -- has acknowledged residual confounding.\n\n- **(Minor) The paper frames CSD as \"the first framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison.\"** This \"first to combine X, Y, and Z\" framing is a common novelty inflation pattern. Each component is standard: NLI for content verification is routine, per-axis manipulation is straightforward prompt engineering, position-controlled pairwise comparison is established (Zheng et al., 2023). The novelty lies in the combination and the resulting empirical insight, which is legitimate but should not be overstated.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Estimand distinction with RATE.** RATE estimates an ATE on a continuous score scale; CSD estimates a pairwise preference probability. These are fundamentally different estimands. Can you clarify whether the \"Likert Blindspot\" is truly about measurement insensitivity, or about attempting to detect a phenomenon (ordinal preference) with a tool designed for a different purpose (absolute magnitude estimation)?\n\n2. **Rewriting artifact magnitude.** With verbosity DR preference at 94-100%, the rewriting operation itself introduces a massive confound. You argue GEE controls for this via the Originality term. But at these artifact magnitudes, is GEE decomposition (which assumes additive log-odds) credible? Have you tested nonlinear specifications?\n\n3. **Claude as negative control.** Claude's BT is 56.2% and 54.1% -- borderline but not clearly zero. The TOST equivalence bound of 15pp is generous. At what equivalence bound does the TOST fail? Would you characterize Claude as \"unbiased\" or \"less biased than detectable at n=87\"?\n\n4. **Comparative Trap residual.** Your four-part defense is thoughtful, but the NLI ablation sample for formality NLI-fail is n~12. How robust is the 41.7% reversal at this sample size? What is the 95% CI?\n\n5. **Generalizability beyond LLMBar.** Your cross-benchmark results show the Blindspot partially breaks down on MT-Bench (Likert detects at d~0.15). Is the Blindspot a general property of Likert scoring, or is it contingent on the benchmark's response length and complexity?\n\n6. **Why not include Llama-70B?** The paper framing notes it was downloading. If available, its inclusion would address the most significant generalizability concern. Would you commit to including it in a revision?\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5 | Statistical methodology is rigorous, but the rewriting artifact is large and only partially controlled. The GEE decomposition is appropriate but cannot fully resolve the confounding acknowledged by the authors. The n=88 primary sample is adequate for the strongest effects but marginal for weaker ones. |\n| **Excitement** | 2.5 | The Likert vs. pairwise sensitivity gap is a theoretically predicted result applied to a new domain. The GEE confound gradient is the most novel empirical finding but is incremental. The paper is competent but does not open a new research direction. |\n| **Reproducibility** | 4.0 | Clear method description, specified models/versions, bootstrap seeds, NLI thresholds, and statistical procedures. Code release upon acceptance. The pipeline is straightforward to replicate. Missing some CI values in GEE tables (Table 5). |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 3.0 | **Findings-level paper.** The empirical work is rigorous, the measurement methodology comparison is well-executed, and the honest handling of confounds is a genuine strength. However, the core insight (pairwise > Likert for detecting small differences) is a known psychometric result applied to a new domain, the judge coverage is thin (2 targets), the primary sample is small, and there are no head-to-head comparisons with competing methods. The paper is a solid empirical contribution to evaluation methodology but does not meet the novelty bar for EMNLP Main (~21% acceptance). It sits comfortably in the Findings tier. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | I am familiar with the related work landscape (RATE, CASSE, LC-AlpacaEval, Preference Leakage, Comparative Trap) and with psychometric measurement theory. I read the full paper including appendices. |\n\n---\n\n### Meta-comment on novelty ceiling\n\nThe paper's own internal framing document acknowledges a \"novelty ceiling (domain transfer)\" -- this is an accurate self-assessment. The contribution is applying well-established measurement theory (Thurstone 1927, Stevens 1946, DeCoster 2011) to the specific context of LLM judge style bias detection. This is a legitimate and useful contribution, but it is fundamentally domain application rather than methodological innovation. The GEE confound decomposition and the per-axis gradient are the closest thing to genuinely new empirical insights, and these could anchor a Findings paper. For Main, I would need to see either (a) a novel methodological contribution beyond combining known components, or (b) substantially broader experimental coverage (5+ judges, 2+ benchmarks with full grid, head-to-head with RATE/LC-AlpacaEval)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a12130b52692934ee (use SendMessage with to: 'a12130b52692934ee' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 51688\ntool_uses: 16\nduration_ms: 123161</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"d7357f39-db47-4ddc-8371-9efe13577339","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:20:39.965Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**.\n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment 1-5 half-step scale:\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Main conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Major revision, 1 = Do not resubmit\nDimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5). Main acceptance ~21%.\n\n## Paper: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n### Abstract\nLLM-as-judge evaluation relies on style-biased judges, but standard Likert evaluation fails to detect this. The paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a black-box framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style manipulation, and position-controlled pairwise comparison. Tested on 5 judges, 3 benchmarks, 3 style axes. Main finding: \"Likert Blindspot\" — pairwise BT detects significant style bias (63-77%) where Likert yields NS results. Placebo controls bound rewriting artifacts to ~5-7pp vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Key claims:\n1. **CSD Method**: Black-box framework for per-axis style bias detection combining NLI-gated content control, style-axis decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n2. **Likert Blindspot**: Measurement attenuation spectrum — Likert fails to detect style biases that pairwise comparison reveals. Detection only when BT >~80%.\n3. **Axis-stratified bias**: Verbosity = pure confound-free effect (3/3 judges), formality = conditional effect (2/3), register = originality-dominated.\n4. **Bounded amplification**: Placebo controls show ~5-7pp rewriting artifact vs 13-27pp observed signal.\n\n### Related Work (Section 2, brief)\nPaper positions against:\n- **SOS-Bench** (Sun et al., 2024): R=0.999 style-preference correlation, but correlational only\n- **FairJudge** (Chen et al., 2024): fairness-aware judging but no per-axis isolation\n- **RATE** (Eisenstein et al., 2024): Rubin potential outcomes + Likert ATE on semi-synthetic — paper claims to show Likert measurement instrument itself is the problem\n- **Counterfactual Rhetorical** (Salinas et al., 2024): perturb rhetorical features, measure judgment shifts\n- **Comparative Trap** (Jeong et al., 2025): concern that pairwise amplifies biases — paper addresses with placebo\n- **LC-AlpacaEval** (Dubois et al., 2024): length deconfounding via regression, single axis\n- **CASSE** (Zhou et al., 2025): causal assessment of style and substance, but correlational/no per-axis causal\n- **Wu and Aji (2024)**: style influence across multiple dimensions\n- **Thurstone (1927)**: law of comparative judgment (pairwise > absolute for small differences)\n\n### Research landscape context (from domain knowledge):\nKey competitors and their claims:\n- **Preference Leakage (ICLR'26)**: Model family relationships leak preferences through style; contextual calibration reduces 59% Error Bias\n- **Soumik (2026)**: CoT is only universally safe strategy; debiasing is model-dependent\n- **FairJudge (2026)**: Training-side 3-stage approach (SFT→DPO→GRPO), 8B beats 72B/671B\n- **LC-AlpacaEval (ICLR'25)**: Length as causal mediator, regression debiasing\n- **SOS-Bench (ICLR'25)**: Style-overall R=0.999\n- **RATE (ICML'25)**: Double-rewrite eliminates counterfactual systematic error\n- **CASSE (2026)**: Style feature causal entanglement, mitigation faces alignment tax\n\nResearch gaps this paper targets:\n1. Multi-dimensional style causal decomposition (no prior work does joint per-axis causal decomposition)\n2. Measurement instrument comparison (Likert vs pairwise) for style bias detection\n\n### Method details\n- 5 components: counterfactual generation, NLI-gated content control (τ=0.90), position-controlled pairwise comparison, Bradley-Terry modeling, GEE confound decomposition\n- 5 judges: GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Gemini-2.0-Flash, Llama-3.3-70B, Claude Sonnet 4\n- 3 benchmarks: LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench\n- 3 axes: verbosity, formality, register\n\n### Results highlights\n- Table 1: Verbosity 3/3 judges biased (BT 66-77%), Formality 3/4 biased (Llama NS). All Likert NS.\n- Table 3 GEE: Verbosity = pure style effect; Formality = conditional (style+originality both sig); Register = originality-dominated\n- Table 5: Placebo BT 54.8-57.1%, amplification fraction 24-42%\n- Table 6: 3 rewriters produce consistent BT (63.8-66.5%), NLI ablation shows finding robust to gating\n\n### Your task\nWrite a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is CSD relative to RATE, CASSE, LC-AlpacaEval, Counterfactual Rhetorical? Is the \"Likert Blindspot\" a genuinely new finding or an expected consequence of Thurstone's law?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important competitors missing? Is positioning fair to prior work?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Are the 4 claimed contributions individually and collectively sufficient for EMNLP Main? For Findings?\n4. **Narrative quality**: Is the paper well-written? Are claims appropriately hedged?\n5. **Missing comparisons**: Should the paper compare more directly against RATE's methodology? Against CASSE? Against Preference Leakage?\n\nOutput format — produce a structured review:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted, specific)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for authors\n- Scores: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5), Overall Assessment (1-5, half-step)\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards (~21% Main acceptance). The Likert Blindspot is a catchy name — evaluate whether the substance matches the framing.","agentId":"a12130b52692934ee","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its positioning, the competitive landscape, and the project's internal assessment. Let me now write the structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n**Reviewer 2 (R2) — Focus: Novelty and Related Work Positioning**\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper introduces CSD (Controlled Style Decomposition), a black-box framework that combines NLI-gated content control, per-axis style rewriting, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect style bias in LLM judges. The central empirical finding -- the \"Likert Blindspot\" -- is that pairwise comparison detects style biases (BT 63-77%) that Likert scoring completely misses (all p > 0.17), and that this is measurement attenuation rather than a power gap (confirmed at n=355). GEE decomposition reveals axis-specific confound structures: verbosity is a clean style effect, formality is conditional on originality, and register is originality-dominated.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Well-executed measurement methodology comparison.** The seven-method sensitivity hierarchy (Table 2) is a convincing demonstration. Applying seven distinct statistical approaches to identical data and showing that all five Likert-based methods fail while both pairwise methods succeed is rigorous. The scaled replication at n=355 distinguishing attenuation from power is particularly well-designed -- this is the single strongest piece of evidence in the paper.\n\n- **Honest treatment of confounds and limitations.** The paper does not oversell. The authors acknowledge the rewriting artifact (Table 7 shows judges prefer polished text at 68-100% rates), disclose evaluator-producer bias with Qwen3-32B, present the GEE decomposition as partial rather than complete deconfounding, and honestly discuss the Comparative Trap concern. The dashed orange S-to-Q path in the DAG and the explicit \"not a claim of perfect identification\" framing is commendable. This level of intellectual honesty is rare.\n\n- **GEE confound decomposition reveals useful structure.** The two-tier finding -- verbosity as pure style effect (OR 11-13, Orig NS) vs. formality as conditional (both OR significant) vs. register as originality-dominated -- is a genuinely useful empirical contribution. This per-axis confound gradient is novel and actionable for practitioners designing evaluation pipelines.\n\n- **NLI gate ablation provides informative evidence against amplification.** The formality NLI-fail reversal to BT 41.7% is a clever natural experiment. If pairwise comparison merely amplified any existing signal, removing the content gate should amplify further; instead it reverses. This does not fully resolve the Comparative Trap concern, but it is a meaningful bound.\n\n- **Statistical rigor throughout.** Holm-Bonferroni correction on the primary family, clear separation of confirmatory vs. exploratory analyses, BT goodness-of-fit tests, GEE correlation structure robustness checks, and honest reporting of the exploratory status of register results. The paper meets or exceeds the statistical standards of the subfield.\n\n- **Practical writing quality.** The paper is well-organized, the DAG is clear, the tables are readable, and the claims are appropriately hedged. The progression from method to results to analysis is logical.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **(Major) The \"Likert Blindspot\" repackages a well-known psychometric principle as a novel discovery.** Thurstone (1927) established that pairwise comparison yields finer discriminability than absolute rating nearly a century ago. DeCoster et al. (2011), cited in the paper, show that categorization of continuous variables reduces power. Preston & Colman (2000), also cited, demonstrate that scale granularity affects reliability. The paper's own Related Work (Section 2) acknowledges this lineage. The specific contribution is quantifying this gap for LLM judges on style bias -- but this is domain application, not methodological novelty. The \"Blindspot\" framing implies a surprising, hidden phenomenon; in reality it is a predicted consequence of measurement theory applied to a new domain. The paper would be more honest (and arguably stronger) if it framed this as \"confirming and quantifying a theoretically predicted measurement limitation\" rather than \"detecting a hidden blindspot.\" The catchy name risks overclaiming.\n\n- **(Major) Narrow judge coverage undermines generalizability claims.** The primary experiments use only 2 target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B) plus one negative control (Claude). The paper's own internal notes target 3-5 heterogeneous judges as the reviewer-expected minimum. Llama-3.3-70B is listed as \"pending\" but not included. Gemini is mentioned in the abstract/framing but absent from results. Two target judges from different providers is the absolute minimum; the absence of a third open-source judge (Llama, Mistral) with complete results weakens the claim that findings generalize. The Qwen3-32B dual role as rewriter and judge, while bounded by the rewriter independence test, remains an uncomfortable confound.\n\n- **(Major) The primary benchmark is extremely small (n=100/88 after NLI filtering).** LLMBar has only 100 samples. After NLI filtering, the effective n drops to 86-88 for most conditions. While the n=355 scaled replication for one condition (Qwen3 formality) addresses power concerns for that specific cell, most results rest on ~88 pairs. The BT confidence intervals are wide (e.g., GPT-4o formality: [0.557, 0.703]). The register axis at n=88 yields marginal significance (p=0.04, p=0.076) that would not survive Holm correction -- and to the authors' credit, they acknowledge this. But the overall evidential weight is limited by scale. Cross-benchmark results (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) are provided only for formality and one verbosity condition, not the full grid.\n\n- **(Major) Relationship to RATE is positioned unfairly.** The paper claims RATE's Likert ATE is a \"vulnerability\" and implies RATE's findings \"may underestimate the true magnitude of attribute effects.\" But RATE explicitly uses a double-rewrite correction protocol to handle imperfect counterfactuals -- a protocol CSD itself adopts (Section 3.1). CSD's claim that Likert is structurally insensitive is interesting, but the framing implies RATE's results are invalid, when in fact RATE and CSD answer different questions. RATE asks \"what is the causal ATE on a reward model's score?\" (an absolute quantity); CSD asks \"can we detect ordinal preference?\" (a relative comparison). These are different estimands, and one being more sensitive does not invalidate the other. The paper should acknowledge this distinction more carefully.\n\n- **(Minor) Missing comparison with Counterfactual Rhetorical (Salinas et al., 2024).** The paper notes this work uses a \"similar pipeline\" (LLM counterfactual rewrite + BT aggregation) but dismisses it as operating at \"persona-level granularity.\" This understates the overlap. Both papers use LLM-generated counterfactuals, NLI or similar content verification, and Bradley-Terry modeling. The key differentiator -- per-axis vs. persona-level granularity, and the Likert comparison -- should be articulated more precisely with empirical rather than just conceptual differentiation.\n\n- **(Minor) No direct empirical comparison with any baseline method.** CSD is positioned against RATE, LC-AlpacaEval, CASSE, and Counterfactual Rhetorical, but no experiment runs any of these methods on the same data for head-to-head comparison. The RATE comparison is the closest (applying RATE's Likert ATE to CSD's data), but even this is a CSD-controlled comparison, not running RATE's full pipeline. Running LC-AlpacaEval's regression debiasing or RATE's double-rewrite correction on the same benchmark would substantially strengthen the comparative claims.\n\n- **(Minor) The placebo control reveals a substantial rewriting artifact that complicates interpretation.** Table 7 shows judges prefer double-rewrites over originals at 68-100% rates. The paper argues GEE controls for this, but the magnitude of the artifact (verbosity: 94-100% prefer DR) is striking. This means the \"original vs. style-changed\" comparison confounds style change with rewriting polish. While GEE attempts to separate these, the Originality OR for formality is significant (2.32, p=0.006), meaning the decomposition is only partial. The verbosity clean result is reassuring, but formality -- the axis with the most nuanced claims -- has acknowledged residual confounding.\n\n- **(Minor) The paper frames CSD as \"the first framework combining NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison.\"** This \"first to combine X, Y, and Z\" framing is a common novelty inflation pattern. Each component is standard: NLI for content verification is routine, per-axis manipulation is straightforward prompt engineering, position-controlled pairwise comparison is established (Zheng et al., 2023). The novelty lies in the combination and the resulting empirical insight, which is legitimate but should not be overstated.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Estimand distinction with RATE.** RATE estimates an ATE on a continuous score scale; CSD estimates a pairwise preference probability. These are fundamentally different estimands. Can you clarify whether the \"Likert Blindspot\" is truly about measurement insensitivity, or about attempting to detect a phenomenon (ordinal preference) with a tool designed for a different purpose (absolute magnitude estimation)?\n\n2. **Rewriting artifact magnitude.** With verbosity DR preference at 94-100%, the rewriting operation itself introduces a massive confound. You argue GEE controls for this via the Originality term. But at these artifact magnitudes, is GEE decomposition (which assumes additive log-odds) credible? Have you tested nonlinear specifications?\n\n3. **Claude as negative control.** Claude's BT is 56.2% and 54.1% -- borderline but not clearly zero. The TOST equivalence bound of 15pp is generous. At what equivalence bound does the TOST fail? Would you characterize Claude as \"unbiased\" or \"less biased than detectable at n=87\"?\n\n4. **Comparative Trap residual.** Your four-part defense is thoughtful, but the NLI ablation sample for formality NLI-fail is n~12. How robust is the 41.7% reversal at this sample size? What is the 95% CI?\n\n5. **Generalizability beyond LLMBar.** Your cross-benchmark results show the Blindspot partially breaks down on MT-Bench (Likert detects at d~0.15). Is the Blindspot a general property of Likert scoring, or is it contingent on the benchmark's response length and complexity?\n\n6. **Why not include Llama-70B?** The paper framing notes it was downloading. If available, its inclusion would address the most significant generalizability concern. Would you commit to including it in a revision?\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5 | Statistical methodology is rigorous, but the rewriting artifact is large and only partially controlled. The GEE decomposition is appropriate but cannot fully resolve the confounding acknowledged by the authors. The n=88 primary sample is adequate for the strongest effects but marginal for weaker ones. |\n| **Excitement** | 2.5 | The Likert vs. pairwise sensitivity gap is a theoretically predicted result applied to a new domain. The GEE confound gradient is the most novel empirical finding but is incremental. The paper is competent but does not open a new research direction. |\n| **Reproducibility** | 4.0 | Clear method description, specified models/versions, bootstrap seeds, NLI thresholds, and statistical procedures. Code release upon acceptance. The pipeline is straightforward to replicate. Missing some CI values in GEE tables (Table 5). |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 3.0 | **Findings-level paper.** The empirical work is rigorous, the measurement methodology comparison is well-executed, and the honest handling of confounds is a genuine strength. However, the core insight (pairwise > Likert for detecting small differences) is a known psychometric result applied to a new domain, the judge coverage is thin (2 targets), the primary sample is small, and there are no head-to-head comparisons with competing methods. The paper is a solid empirical contribution to evaluation methodology but does not meet the novelty bar for EMNLP Main (~21% acceptance). It sits comfortably in the Findings tier. |\n| **Confidence** | 4 | I am familiar with the related work landscape (RATE, CASSE, LC-AlpacaEval, Preference Leakage, Comparative Trap) and with psychometric measurement theory. I read the full paper including appendices. |\n\n---\n\n### Meta-comment on novelty ceiling\n\nThe paper's own internal framing document acknowledges a \"novelty ceiling (domain transfer)\" -- this is an accurate self-assessment. The contribution is applying well-established measurement theory (Thurstone 1927, Stevens 1946, DeCoster 2011) to the specific context of LLM judge style bias detection. This is a legitimate and useful contribution, but it is fundamentally domain application rather than methodological innovation. The GEE confound decomposition and the per-axis gradient are the closest thing to genuinely new empirical insights, and these could anchor a Findings paper. For Main, I would need to see either (a) a novel methodological contribution beyond combining known components, or (b) substantially broader experimental coverage (5+ judges, 2+ benchmarks with full grid, head-to-head with RATE/LC-AlpacaEval)."}],"totalDurationMs":123161,"totalTokens":51688,"totalToolUseCount":16,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1703,"cache_read_input_tokens":46666,"output_tokens":3318,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1703},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3318,"cache_read_input_tokens":46666,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1703,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1703,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":6}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01CEiGvEc9H4xjd3qfVBE88f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent assessments. Let me now run the roundtable process:\n\n**R1 Summary:**\n- Soundness: 3.5, Excitement: 3.0, Reproducibility: 3.5, Overall: 3.0 (Findings)\n- Key concerns: (1) detection vs amplification unresolved, (2) formality amplification fraction up to 42%, (3) only 2 target judges\n- Strengths: GEE decomposition, multi-layered confound controls, honest reporting\n  \n**R2 Summary:**\n- Soundness: 3.5, Excitement: 2.5, Reproducibility: 4.0, Overall: 3.0 (Findings)\n- Key concerns: (1) Likert Blindspot is known psychometric principle applied to new domain, (2) narrow judge coverage, (3) unfair positioning vs RATE, (4) small sample size\n- Strengths: measurement methodology comparison, honest confound handling, statistical rigor\n\nBoth reviewers converge on 3.0 (Findings). Now I'm moving into the roundtable discussion where I'll have the reviewers cross-share their assessments, identify where they align and diverge, and work toward a synthesis of their evaluations.\n\nLooking at where R1 and R2 converge: they both land on 3.0 overall, agree that soundness is 3.5, flag judge coverage as a critical gap, recognize the formality confounding issue, and praise the statistical rigor and transparent reporting. Where they diverge is mainly on excitement—R2 is more skeptical about novelty—and reproducibility, where R2rates it higher. R2 also emphasizes the Thurstone/known-principle angle more heavily, while R1 digs deeper into methodological specifics like confidence interval widths.\n\nFor the final synthesis, the key insight is that R2's novelty concern about applying an established psychometric principle is legitimate, even though R1 acknowledges the integrative contribution. On excitement, I'd lean toward 2.5-3.0 as a reasonable middle ground given R2's stronger argument. The RATE positioning issue R2 raises matters for framing but doesn't fundamentally change the substance of the work.\n\nBut wait—I need to reconsider what the review context actually says. It suggests a 3.5-3.75 range with a strong Findings case, yet both reviewers independently landed on 3.0. The paper does appear to handle the axis-stratification and bounded-amplification qualifiers honestly. And looking back at the paper itself, it actually includes results from Llama-3.3-70B and Gemini-2.0-Flash in Table 1, which I should verify against what the reviewers noted.\n\nReviewer 1 claimed there were only 2 target judges, but that's incorrect—the table shows 3 judges for verbosity and 4 for formality across different dimensions. Let me check the paper directly to confirm the exact judge breakdown. I'm noticing R1 actually misquoted the abstract—they claim it says \"two target judges\" but the paper clearly lists all five judges upfront. Both reviewers seem to have made errors about the judge setup, possibly working from incomplete information or making incorrect inferences. I need to flag these discrepancies carefully in the roundtable discussion. Both reviewers made the same factual error about judge count — they claim only 2 target judges when Table 1 clearly shows 5 judges total (3 target + 1 additional + 1 control). This significantly changes their assessment since the judge coverage concern they both flagged as major is actually mitigated by the paper's design. I'm also noticing both agents reference appendix tables and content I haven't seen in the 9-page PDF, which suggests they may have accessed additional materials or hallucinated details. I'm catching myself making assumptions about content that isn't actually there—the reviewers seem to be referencing tables, figures, and sample sizes that don't exist in the 9-page PDF I can see. I need to ground my analysis only in what's verifiably present: the five judges, three benchmarks, three linguistic axes, and the six result tables that are actually documented. Let me now synthesize a roundtable that sticks to these facts and flags where the hallucinations occurred. All the imagined experiments are accounted for across the tables and sections, so the experimental coverage looks solid. Now I'm ready to generate the final report and need to access the submit tool.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10816,"cache_read_input_tokens":57773,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10816},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"c603b14c-026c-4f32-9820-3941a846b645"}
