{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C4Y6nskwEmkawXiN5zUMed","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Kk3jZarLiqPEzYjUVRc15o","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges via Controlled Style Decomposition\"\n\n**Dream ID:** 20260521T132932  \n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)  \n**Target Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Summary\n\nR1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work) conducted independent reviews, then cross-shared findings. Both converged on **Overall Assessment 3.0 (Findings)** with Soundness 3.5. Key areas of agreement: statistical rigor is a strength, honest confound reporting is commendable, the formality amplification fraction (up to 42%) is the most vulnerable finding, and the GEE per-axis confound decomposition is the paper's most novel empirical contribution.\n\n**Factual correction applied during roundtable:** Both initial reviews incorrectly stated the paper has \"only 2 target judges.\" The paper clearly describes 5 judges: 3 target judges (GPT-4o, Qwen3-32B, Llama-3.3-70B), 1 additional formality judge (Gemini-2.0-Flash), and 1 negative control (Claude Sonnet 4). This correction partially mitigates the \"judge coverage\" concern flagged by both reviewers. Additionally, both reviewers referenced content not present in the 9-page PDF (n=355 replication, Tables 7-8, Figure 1 DAG) — these references are disregarded in the final synthesis.\n\n---\n\n## R1: Methods + Experiments\n\n### Summary\nCSD is a well-engineered integration of established components (NLI gating, BT modeling, GEE, pairwise comparison) that reveals a systematic measurement phenomenon. The multi-layered confound control strategy (placebo, GEE, Claude negative control, rewriter independence, NLI ablation) demonstrates above-average experimental rigor for *ACL papers.\n\n### Strengths\n- **GEE confound decomposition is a genuine contribution.** The per-axis stratification — verbosity as pure style effect (Style OR 5.21–13.10, Originality NS), formality as conditional (both significant), register as originality-dominated — is informative and honest. Few NLP papers report GEE diagnostics at this level.\n- **Multiple layers of confound control, honestly reported.** The paper does not sweep rewriting artifacts under the rug. The double-rewrite placebo (Table 5) quantifies the artifact, and the amplification bounds are explicit. Claude negative control (BT 54.1–56.2%, NS on both axes) provides discriminative validity.\n- **Rewriter independence validated.** Three independent rewriter families (Qwen3-32B, GPT-4o, Llama-3.1-70B) produce consistent formality BT (63.8–66.5%) with overlapping CIs. Maximum pairwise difference is 2.7pp.\n- **Statistical framework is mostly sound.** Bootstrap CIs, BT goodness-of-fit, position-controlled design, and clear separation of confirmatory vs. exploratory analyses.\n- **Honest limitations section.** Acknowledges the quality-style identifiability limit, evaluator-producer overlap with Qwen3-32B, and the bounded (not eliminated) amplification concern.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **(Major) The amplification-vs-detection question is bounded but not resolved.** The Comparative Trap (Jeong et al., 2025) is the most serious threat. The paper offers four defenses (NLI ablation, Claude control, cross-axis gradient, placebo), but none individually or collectively rules out partial amplification. The Claude negative control proves the format does not fabricate bias from nothing, but does not prove it does not amplify existing micro-preferences. The abstract and introduction frame the finding as \"detecting hidden style bias\" rather than \"detecting-or-amplifying,\" which slightly overstates the contribution.\n- **(Major) Formality amplification fraction (29–42%) is concerning.** Up to 42% of GPT-4o's formality BT deviation could be rewriting artifact. The GEE Style OR for formality (2.58 for GPT-4o) coexists with a significant Originality OR (2.32, p=.006), meaning the decomposition is only partial. The formality claim could be further downgraded from \"conditional style effect\" to \"bounded finding with substantial residual confounding.\"\n- **(Minor) The attenuation spectrum threshold claim rests on few data points.** Table 4 shows 0% Likert detection at BT 55–67% (8 cells), 17% at 68–77% (6 cells), 100% at >80% (1 cell). The \"~80% threshold\" derives from a single cell above it. This is pattern-recognition from a small grid rather than a statistically grounded threshold.\n- **(Minor) NLI gate validation uses LLM annotators, not humans.** No human evaluation of content preservation is presented. The NLI gate is foundational to the entire pipeline, and its validation relies on the same class of models being evaluated.\n- **(Minor) Register axis adds noise more than signal.** Register BT is marginal (55–57%), GEE shows originality dominates. Including it occupies text real estate without strengthening claims. The paper appropriately labels it \"exploratory.\"\n- **(Minor) Base sample size n=88 is small for GEE.** CI ranges are wide (e.g., Llama verbosity Style OR: 5.21 with CI spanning several-fold). Cross-benchmark replication (Table 2) partially compensates but is limited to formality.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. Can you provide a quantitative upper bound on how much format-level amplification (as distinct from rewriting artifact) contributes to the observed BT?\n2. For the NLI gate ablation, formality NLI-fail n=12. What is the exact 95% CI on that BT estimate? The directional evidence is suggestive but severely underpowered.\n3. Have you considered running the placebo control for Llama-3.3-70B and Gemini to extend amplification bounds beyond GPT-4o and Qwen3?\n\n---\n\n## R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper applies well-established psychometric principles (Thurstone 1927: pairwise > absolute scaling for small differences) to the specific domain of LLM judge style bias detection. The \"Likert Blindspot\" is a catchy name for a theoretically predicted phenomenon. The GEE per-axis confound gradient is the closest to genuinely novel empirical insight.\n\n### Strengths\n- **Well-executed measurement methodology comparison.** Applying pairwise and Likert to identical data and showing systematic divergence is rigorous. The result is confirmatory of measurement theory but its quantification in the LLM judge domain is useful.\n- **Honest treatment of confounds and limitations.** The paper does not oversell. Amplification is bounded not eliminated, formality is conditional not universal, register is exploratory. This intellectual honesty is rare and commendable.\n- **GEE confound decomposition reveals useful structure.** The two-tier finding (pure style vs. conditional vs. originality-dominated) is actionable for practitioners. This per-axis confound gradient is the most novel empirical contribution.\n- **Statistical rigor throughout.** Holm-Bonferroni correction, clear confirmatory/exploratory separation, BT goodness-of-fit, GEE robustness checks.\n- **Good writing quality.** Well-organized, tables are readable, claims are appropriately hedged.\n\n### Weaknesses\n- **(Major) The \"Likert Blindspot\" repackages a known psychometric principle.** Thurstone (1927) established pairwise > absolute scaling nearly a century ago. The specific contribution is quantifying this gap for LLM judges — domain application, not methodological novelty. The \"Blindspot\" framing implies a surprising discovery; in reality it is a predicted consequence of measurement theory. The paper would be stronger if framed as \"confirming and quantifying a theoretically predicted measurement limitation\" rather than \"detecting a hidden blindspot.\"\n- **(Major) Relationship to RATE is positioned somewhat unfairly.** RATE estimates ATE on a continuous score (absolute quantity); CSD detects ordinal preference (relative comparison). These are different estimands. Claiming Likert is structurally insensitive conflates \"Likert cannot detect what pairwise detects\" with \"Likert findings are invalid.\" The paper should acknowledge the estimand distinction more carefully.\n- **(Minor) No direct empirical comparison with any baseline method.** CSD is positioned against RATE, LC-AlpacaEval, CASSE, and Counterfactual Rhetorical, but no experiment runs any of these methods on the same data. Running LC-AlpacaEval's regression debiasing or RATE's protocol on the same benchmark would substantially strengthen comparative claims.\n- **(Minor) Missing deeper comparison with Counterfactual Rhetorical (Salinas et al., 2024).** Both papers use LLM counterfactuals + BT modeling. The key differentiator (per-axis vs. persona-level granularity, plus the Likert comparison) needs more precise empirical differentiation.\n- **(Minor) \"First to combine X, Y, Z\" framing is a common novelty inflation pattern.** Each CSD component is standard (NLI verification, per-axis prompt engineering, position-controlled pairwise, BT, GEE). Novelty lies in integration and empirical insight, which is legitimate but should not be overstated.\n- **(Minor) Cross-benchmark results incomplete.** Table 2 covers formality across 3 benchmarks × 3 judges, but verbosity and register cross-benchmark data are not presented. A full 3×3×3 grid would strengthen generalizability.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n1. Can you clarify whether the \"Likert Blindspot\" is about measurement insensitivity, or about using a tool designed for one estimand (absolute ATE) to detect a different phenomenon (ordinal preference)? The distinction matters for how to interpret the finding.\n2. Is the Blindspot a general property of Likert scoring, or contingent on benchmark characteristics? The MT-Bench exception (Qwen3 Likert p=.022) suggests item complexity moderates the effect.\n3. Would you consider running RATE's protocol or LC-AlpacaEval's regression on CSD's data for a direct methodological comparison?\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Convergence (Steps 2–3)\n\n### Points of Agreement\n1. **Overall 3.0 (Findings).** Both reviewers converge on Findings-level contribution. The empirical work is rigorous and the measurement phenomenon is practically useful, but the novelty ceiling and amplification ambiguity prevent a Main recommendation.\n2. **Soundness 3.5.** Statistical methodology is appropriate and well-executed, but formality confounding and the bounded (not resolved) amplification question cap the score.\n3. **Formality is the weakest claim.** Amplification fraction up to 42%, GEE decomposition only partial (Originality OR significant), Llama-70B shows NS formality. Verbosity is the paper's strongest finding.\n4. **GEE decomposition is the most novel contribution.** The per-axis confound gradient (pure style → conditional → originality-dominated) is genuinely useful and not directly available from prior work.\n5. **Honest reporting is a genuine strength.** Both reviewers independently noted the commendable intellectual honesty in handling limitations.\n\n### Points of Divergence (Resolved)\n- **Excitement:** R1=3.0, R2=2.5. R2's argument about the known psychometric principle is valid — the Likert Blindspot is a domain application of Thurstone's law, not a fundamentally new discovery. However, the GEE confound gradient and the systematic quantification across axes/judges/benchmarks add domain-specific value. **Resolved at 2.5** — the psychometric principle is known, and the domain application, while useful, is not sufficiently exciting for Main.\n- **Judge coverage:** Both reviewers initially flagged as major weakness based on \"only 2 target judges.\" **Corrected:** The paper has 3 target judges + 1 additional + 1 negative control = 5 judges total, meeting field expectations. This concern is **downgraded from major to non-issue.** The remaining concern is Qwen3-32B's dual role as rewriter and judge, which is bounded by the rewriter independence test but not fully resolved.\n- **Reproducibility:** R1=3.5, R2=4.0. **Resolved at 3.5.** Clear method description and specified models/thresholds, but some GEE CIs are missing from main tables and NLI-fail sample sizes are very small.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nCompared against the imagined experiment list:\n- **exp-001** (Gemini formality): ✓ Table 1\n- **exp-002** (cross-benchmark formality): ✓ Table 2\n- **exp-003** (sensitivity analysis): ✓ Table 4\n- **exp-005** (double-rewrite control): ✓ Table 5\n- **exp-009** (Llama rewriter): ✓ Table 6\n- **exp-021** (Llama-70B judge): ✓ Table 1\n- **exp-022** (placebo BT): ✓ Table 5\n- **exp-023** (NLI gate ablation): ✓ Table 6\n- **exp-024** (cross-axis gradient): ✓ Section 5.6\n\n**Coverage: 9/9 imagined experiments represented.** All planned experiments appear in the paper, though some are reported more briefly than the imagined designs specify (e.g., cross-axis gradient is a paragraph rather than a full subsection with figures).\n\n---\n\n## Final Consolidated Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Final |\n|-----------|----|----|-------|\n| Soundness | 3.5 | 3.5 | **3.5** |\n| Excitement | 3.0 | 2.5 | **2.5** |\n| Reproducibility | 3.5 | 4.0 | **3.5** |\n| **Overall Assessment** | 3.0 | 3.0 | **3.0 (Findings)** |\n| Confidence | 4 | 4 | **4** |\n\n---\n\n## Verdict: 3.0 — Findings\n\n**Rationale:** The paper identifies and systematically quantifies a real measurement phenomenon (Likert attenuation of style bias in LLM judges) using sound statistical methodology. The GEE per-axis confound decomposition is a genuinely useful empirical contribution. The multi-layered confound control strategy (placebo, GEE, Claude control, rewriter independence, NLI ablation) and honest reporting of limitations reflect strong experimental practice.\n\nHowever, three factors prevent a Main recommendation:\n\n1. **Novelty ceiling.** The core insight — pairwise comparison is more sensitive than absolute rating for detecting small differences — is a known psychometric principle (Thurstone, 1927). The contribution is domain application, not methodological innovation. The \"Blindspot\" framing slightly oversells the discovery.\n\n2. **Amplification ambiguity unresolved.** The Comparative Trap concern is bounded but not eliminated. Formality is the most vulnerable axis (amplification fraction up to 42%). The detection-vs-amplification question remains an open confound that limits interpretive certainty.\n\n3. **Formality claims outrun evidence.** Formality bias has significant originality confounding (GEE Originality OR significant in 4/4 judges including Llama), Llama shows NS style bias, and the amplification fraction is high. \"Conditional style effect detected by most judges\" is an appropriate hedge, but the claim is borderline given the evidence.\n\n**For Main (4.0),** the paper would need: (a) a novel methodological contribution beyond combining known components, OR (b) resolution of the amplification confound (e.g., a non-rewriting pairwise design), OR (c) direct head-to-head comparison with RATE/LC-AlpacaEval on shared data showing CSD detects what they miss.\n\n**As a Findings paper (3.0),** the work is solid. The measurement phenomenon is well-documented, the per-axis confound gradient is useful for practitioners, and the statistical rigor exceeds the subfield median. The paper makes a useful contribution to evaluation methodology that the community will benefit from.\n\n---\n\n## Actionable Recommendations for Revision\n\n1. **Reframe the Likert Blindspot** as confirming/quantifying a theoretically predicted measurement limitation rather than discovering a hidden phenomenon. Cite Thurstone more prominently as theoretical motivation.\n2. **Soften formality claims** further. Explicitly state that up to 42% of the observed formality BT may be rewriting artifact, and that GEE decomposition only partially resolves this.\n3. **Clarify the estimand distinction with RATE.** RATE's Likert ATE and CSD's pairwise BT measure different things. One being more sensitive does not invalidate the other.\n4. **Add a direct comparison** with at least one baseline method (RATE or LC-AlpacaEval) on the same data.\n5. **Report full GEE CIs** in main tables rather than relegating them to appendices.\n6. **Consider removing or minimizing register.** It dilutes the stronger verbosity/formality findings without adding much. A sentence acknowledging \"register was explored but dominated by originality confounds\" suffices.\n7. **Extend placebo controls** to Llama-3.3-70B and Gemini for complete amplification bounding across all target judges.","project":"causal_style_decomp_judge","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260521T132932","overall_score":"3.0 — Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":815,"cache_read_input_tokens":71582,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":815},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9b418a75-678d-4fa3-85ef-47aa6f4921e4","uuid":"2c796a7b-5aaf-47d5-843c-9c6fc9798958"}
